BARK Indexing
=============

BARK is an index access method modeled on nbtree and meant as its
successor; BARK-Design.mediawiki at the top of the tree records its
contracts, decisions and roadmap, and doc/src/sgml/bark.sgml is the user
manual.  It is a Lehman and Yao B-tree over btree operator classes, with
nbtree's suffix truncation, heap TID order among equal keys, bottom-up
deletion and deduplication-before-split, and it adds what nbtree cannot
store: keys of any size (OVERSIZED entries on overflow chains), sorted
duplicates in two forms (LIST and sbm-compressed POSTING entries), leaf
prefix compression, nearest-neighbor (KNN) ordering, and, being built now,
operator classes that extract several keys from one value (multikey).
This README describes the code as built: the on-disk format defined in
src/include/access/bark.h and barkxlog.h, and the algorithms that keep it
correct.  Where BARK does what nbtree does, the text says so and names the
nbtree function, and the nbtree README is the longer explanation.

The code
--------

  bark.c          the handler (barkhandler, the IndexAmRoutine), the
                  reloptions, VACUUM (bark_vacuum_scan, bark_vacuum_page,
                  bark_delete_empty_leaf, bark_pendingfsm_finalize), the
                  cost estimate (barkcostestimate), amgettreeheight
  barkinsert.c    insert (bark_insert, bark_insert_entries,
                  bark_insert_key), the uniqueness check (bark_check_unique),
                  coalescing into LIST and POSTING entries
                  (bark_coalesce_list), the rightmost-leaf fast path
                  (bark_fastpath_leaf), page splits (bark_split,
                  bark_insert_parent, bark_getstackbuf, bark_finish_split,
                  bark_new_root), the first root of an empty index
                  (bark_create_root_leaf), suffix truncation
                  (bark_truncate_pivot), the WAL records of inserts, and
                  amcomparedatums (barkcomparedatums, "HOT updates")
  barksearch.c    descent (bark_search, bark_search_bound, bark_descend,
                  bark_moveright, bark_binsrch), comparison of a scan bound
                  with an entry (bark_compare_bound), the root cache in
                  rd_amcache (bark_get_amcache, bark_get_root)
  barkscan.c      ordinary scans (bark_beginscan, bark_rescan,
                  bark_gettuple, bark_getbitmap): bounds (bark_make_bound),
                  the page read (bark_readpage), stepping and re-descent
                  (bark_readnextpage, bark_lock_and_validate_left), required
                  array keys, skip scan, the per-page satisfied-key test,
                  dead-entry hints (bark_killitems), mark/restore and
                  parallel scans
  barkknn.c       ordered-operator (KNN) scans, ORDER BY col <~> const
  barksort.c      CREATE INDEX (bark_build): heap scan, tuplesort, parallel
                  build, the bottom-up load (bark_load) and the second pass
                  for oversized keys; ambuildempty (bark_buildempty)
  barkdelete.c    simple and bottom-up deletion (bark_simple_delete,
                  bark_bottomup_delete, which share bark_delete_pass), the
                  merge of a leaf's equal-key entries before a split
                  (bark_merge_page),
                  and the re-forming of LIST and POSTING entries that both
                  share with VACUUM (bark_reform_entry)
  barkutils.c     comparison (bark_build_keyinfo, bark_compare_itups,
                  bark_compare_itups_tid, bark_keep_natts), LIST and POSTING
                  entries (bark_form_list, bark_form_posting,
                  bark_form_entry, bark_entry_add_tid, bark_entry_swap_tid,
                  bark_entry_cut), OVERSIZED entries and overflow chains,
                  leaf prefix coding, page allocation (bark_get_free_page),
                  multikey key extraction and markers, the meta page's
                  multikey fields, the KNN distance functions, and the
                  strategy translation the planner uses
  barksplitloc.c  the split point (bark_findsplitloc, bark_singleval_cut)
  barkxlog.c      WAL redo (bark_redo) and bark_mask
  barkvalidate.c  operator class validation (barkvalidate) and
                  barkadjustmembers
  barkverify.c    the checks of one page by itself (bark_page_verify),
                  which amcheck and the cassert page validator share
  ../rmgrdesc/barkdesc.c   WAL record descriptions for pg_waldump

amcheck's checks are contrib/amcheck/verify_bark.c, pageinspect's
functions contrib/pageinspect/barkfuncs.c, and pgstatbarkindex is in
contrib/pgstattuple/pgstatindex.c.

The main data structures, all in bark.h unless noted:

  BarkPageOpaqueData   a page's special space (see "Page structure")
  BarkMetaPageData     block 0
  BarkDeletedPageData  a deleted page's safexid
  BarkOverflowRef      what an OVERSIZED entry keeps inline
  BarkKeyInfo          per-index comparison state: each key column's
                       support function 1, collation and ASC/DESC and NULLS
                       options, built by bark_build_keyinfo
  BarkStackData        a descent's path, for inserting downlinks
  BarkAmCache          rd_amcache: the root block and level, the extracted
                       column, the multikey flag, the markers seen and the
                       level-1 entry table (BarkL1Slot)
  BarkInsertState      (barkinsert.c) ii_AmCache: what every insert of a
                       statement reuses (see "Inserts"), and
                       barkcomparedatums too
  BarkScanBound        a scan's bound on the leading columns, which the
                       descent and the leaf binary search compare against
  BarkScanOpaqueData   a scan's state; BarkScanPosData the copy of one
                       leaf's matching items, as nbtree's BTScanPosData
  BarkArrayKeyState    one ScalarArrayOp key, its elements sorted in the
                       column's index order
  BarkKnnScanState     a KNN scan's two cursors (BarkKnnCursor)
  BarkParallelScanDescData   the shared cursor of a parallel scan
  BarkBuildState, BarkPageState   (barksort.c) CREATE INDEX's state and
                       its per-level page under construction
  BarkItemBuf          workspace for BarkPageGetItem (see "Leaf prefix
                       compression")

Page structure
--------------

BARK borrows nbtree's Lehman & Yao page structure so the high-concurrency
search and split algorithm can be shared.  Every page is a standard
PostgreSQL page whose special space holds a BarkPageOpaqueData:

    bark_prev / bark_next   right-linked sibling chain (BARK_P_NONE at edges)
    bark_level              0 for leaf pages, increasing toward the root
                            (16 bits)
    bark_cycleid            vacuum cycle ID of the latest leaf split, or 0
                            (16 bits; see "VACUUM" below)
    bark_flags              page role: BARK_LEAF, BARK_ROOT, BARK_META,
                            BARK_DELETED, BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT,
                            BARK_OVERFLOW, BARK_PREFIX,
                            BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED (see "Version tags"),
                            and the hint
                            BARK_HAS_GARBAGE (see "Dead-entry hints and
                            simple deletion").  BARK_HALF_DEAD is defined,
                            and readers treat a half-dead page as nbtree
                            treats one, but no code sets it: page deletion
                            is one step (see "Page deletion and
                            recycling")
    bark_page_id            BARK_PAGE_ID (0xFF84), so external tools can
                            tell BARK pages from nbtree pages: nbtree keeps
                            its vacuum cycle ID in the last two bytes of the
                            special space, and those never exceed
                            MAX_BT_CYCLE_ID (0xFF7F); hash, GiST, SP-GiST and
                            bloom use 0xFF80-0xFF83

As in nbtree, each page carries a high key (an upper bound on the keys the
page may hold) and a right-link to its sibling, which together let a scan
detect and recover from a concurrent split without holding read locks across
pages.

Leaf prefix compression
-----------------------

With the prefix_compression reloption (default off), a leaf whose index has a
varlena first key column may be flagged BARK_PREFIX.  Such a leaf holds a
PREFIX item right after its high key (at BarkPagePrefixOff; the data items
start one offset later, at BarkPageFirstDataKey): an IndexTupleData header,
with t_tid zero and t_info set to the item's size, then 1..126 bytes of
prefix.  Each SINGLE, LIST and POSTING entry with a non-NULL first column
stores that column coded: a varlena whose payload's first byte gives how
many bytes of the prefix the value starts with (0..126), the rest being the
value's remaining bytes; the value's varlena header is recomputed on decode
(1-byte when it fits, else 4-byte).  A value compressed inline, or with a
header not in that form, is stored raw (count 127, then the value with its
header).  The bytes after the first column are moved to keep their
alignment, or left out when they are only the key's closing zero padding.
The format comment at BARK_PREFIX in bark.h has the details.  OVERSIZED
entries, NULL first columns, high keys and pivots are stored as on any page.

Every read of a leaf's data items goes through BarkPageGetItem, which
decodes on a BARK_PREFIX page, so comparisons always see the real datum and
any collation works.  High keys, and internal-page items that a caller
changes in place, are read with PageGetItem.  Coding needs only the page's prefix and the entry, not the catalog,
so WAL redo can repeat it (ADD_TID redo decodes, adds the TID, and codes
again); the other records carry coded bytes and redo adds them as they are.

The prefix is chosen only when a leaf is written whole, and never changes:
the build gives a leaf its first item's leading bytes when the previous
leaf's items shared at least 4 of its own on average (the first leaf always
gets one), and each half of a leaf split takes its first item's bytes, cut
to the longest share, when its items share at least 4 bytes on average.
Because each entry records its own share, an entry that shares less (an
insert outside the page's original range, say) is still stored correctly,
so no insert, VACUUM or change of the key range ever forces a page rewrite.
A coded entry can be up to 8 bytes larger than the plain one (the count
byte crossing an alignment boundary), so every free-space test before
placing an entry on a leaf uses bark_coded_size.  A split sizes items as
they are coded on the original page; a half that does not fit with its new
prefix is laid out plain, or, if that does not fit either, with the original
page's prefix, against which it fits by construction.  The split record
says which halves have a prefix (XLH_BARK_SPLIT_LPREFIX / _RPREFIX).

Inserts
-------

bark_insert (barkinsert.c) is aminsert.  It forms the row's index tuple at
full size with bark_form_full_tuple, which, unlike index_form_tuple, does
not cap the size: an entry too large for a page becomes OVERSIZED only when
it is placed (see "Entry encoding").  An index with an extracted column
goes through bark_insert_row_keys instead (see "Multikey keys").  Every
entry goes through bark_insert_entries, which positions it, and
bark_insert_key, which places it on the leaf found.

What every insert of one statement would otherwise rebuild is kept in
indexInfo->ii_AmCache, in ii_Context, for the life of the IndexInfo
(BarkInsertState, bark_insert_state): the BarkKeyInfo, whether the index
coalesces (non-unique and bark_allequalimage, which takes a catalog lookup
and a support-function call per key column), the extracted column, and a
per-row memory context for multikey rows.  GIN keeps its GinState the same
way; nbtree reads its allequalimage from the cached meta page instead.

Positioning.  An insert descends with (key, heap TID) to the one leaf the
entry belongs on (bark_search with a scantid; see "Heap TID order"), write
locking it, finishing on the way any incomplete split it meets.  A unique
check descends on the key alone instead (see "Uniqueness").  Before
descending for a row's first entry, the insert tries the rightmost-leaf
fast path, bark_fastpath_leaf, nbtree's _bt_search_insert: see "Heap TID
order".  The insert then calls CheckForSerializableConflictIn for the leaf,
once for each leaf a row's entries go on.

Placing (bark_insert_key, the per-key function every key of a row uses).
On the write-locked leaf, at the offset bark_leaf_insert_off found:

  1. In an index that coalesces, and for a key that is not oversized,
     bark_coalesce_list tries to add the heap TID to the equal-key entry
     just before the offset, or divide that entry around it (see "Heap TID
     order").  A coalesce that cannot be done in place and needs a split
     first runs the merge pass (see "Merging a page's entries before a
     split") and tries again.
  2. Otherwise the entry goes in as a SINGLE (or an OVERSIZED entry, whose
     overflow chain bark_leaf_page_entry writes first) when it fits in
     bark_leaf_free_space.
  3. If it does not fit: bottom-up deletion, when the executor says the
     index is unchanged (see "Bottom-up deletion"); the single-value cut
     when the page holds one key at the end of its run
     (bark_singleval_cut, see "Page splits"); the merge pass; and only then
     the split (bark_split).  Simple deletion of the entries scans marked
     dead (see "Dead-entry hints and simple deletion") comes before all of
     these, and before a divided entry's split.

A fast-path insert has no stack and so never splits: where it would need
to, bark_insert_key returns BARK_PLACE_RETRY, the leaf is forgotten as the
target and the insert descends.  Each change is one WAL record (see
"WAL").

Uniqueness
----------

bark_check_unique is nbtree's _bt_check_unique.  The insert descends on the
key alone (no heap TID, nextkey false) to the first leaf that can hold the
key; the entries of the key, any of which may be the live one, start there,
at the first entry not less than the key, and the check scans right from
there while keys are equal, keeping the first leaf write-locked throughout
and share-locking each later leaf before releasing the one before it.  For each equal entry it
fetches the heap tuple with SnapshotDirty (table_fetch_tid):

  * a tuple dead to everyone is no conflict;
  * UNIQUE_CHECK_EXISTING skips the entry that is the row itself, and the
    insert then adds nothing (the deferred recheck, unique_key_recheck);
  * a live or in-progress match under UNIQUE_CHECK_PARTIAL (deferrable
    constraints and speculative insertion) sets is_unique false and returns;
  * a match whose inserting or deleting transaction is still running
    returns its XID, and SnapshotDirty's speculativeToken when the inserter
    is a speculative insertion (INSERT ... ON CONFLICT); bark_insert_entries
    releases the leaf and waits, with SpeculativeInsertionWait or
    XactLockTableWait, then descends and checks again;
  * a committed live match raises the unique violation.

When the first leaf holds no entry of the key, the check returns the offset
its binary search found, which is the insert offset for any heap TID, so an
insert that stays on that leaf skips a second search, as _bt_check_unique
saves its bounds for _bt_findinsertloc.  After the check, the insert steps
right, locking each right sibling before releasing the leaf, to the leaf
its heap TID belongs on (bark_insert_stepright, nbtree's _bt_stepright); it
rarely moves at all.

A key with a NULL in any key column is not checked, and is unique, unless
the index is NULLS NOT DISTINCT; then it is checked as any key is, since
bark_compare_itups treats two NULLs in a column as equal.  CREATE INDEX of a
unique index checks each sorted live tuple against the previous live one by
the same rule (bark_load), and its second pass checks live oversized keys by
inserting them with UNIQUE_CHECK_YES; rows the table AM passes as not alive
are indexed but not checked (see "CREATE INDEX").  A unique index never
coalesces, so it holds only SINGLE and OVERSIZED entries.  Whether it is
unique is the catalog's indisunique (bark_index_coalesces), not the
IndexInfo's ii_Unique, which says only whether to enforce the constraint
now: CLUSTER, VACUUM FULL and REPACK rebuild with ii_Unique cleared
(reindex_index's skip_constraint_checks) and still index the dead
duplicates an older snapshot sees.  bark_index_check reports a LIST or
POSTING entry in a unique index as corruption.

A unique BARK index can be an ON CONFLICT arbiter, a foreign key's
referenced index and a replica identity, but no constraint can use one
(parse_utilcmd.c accepts only DEFAULT_INDEX_TYPE for ADD CONSTRAINT ...
USING INDEX, and CREATE TABLE's constraints build btree), so
UNIQUE_CHECK_PARTIAL is reached only from speculative insertion.

Page splits and the incomplete-split flag
-----------------------------------------

A split is two WAL records, as in nbtree.  The first rebuilds the left page,
fills the new right page, and links it in; the left page is flagged
BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT.  The second inserts the right page's downlink into the
parent and clears the flag.  The splitting backend keeps the left page
write-locked from before the first record until after the second, releasing
it only once it holds the parent's write lock and has written the downlink
(the right page is released as soon as the first record is written).  Locks
are taken child before parent at every level.

So a backend that sees BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT under a page lock is looking at a
split nobody is completing: an error or crash interrupted it.  An insert's
descent finishes every such split it reaches, at any level, before it moves
right past it or descends from it (bark_finish_split, with the same locking),
as nbtree's _bt_moveright does.  Merely moving right would not be enough: the
right half has no downlink, so inserts that only ever land on the right half
would split it with no parent entry to insert after.  Every descent, for read
or write, also moves right past pages flagged BARK_DELETED or BARK_HALF_DEAD,
which are never the page a key belongs on.

Inserting a downlink finds the parent by matching the child's block number in
the parent level, starting at the descent's stack entry and moving right,
rather than by key (bark_getstackbuf, after _bt_getstackbuf).  The new
downlink goes immediately after the one to its left sibling.  A split of a
root page that no longer is the root (another backend added a level) finds
its parent from the leftmost page of the next level up.

An empty index (one whose meta page names no root, which only an unlogged
index reset after a crash can be) gets an empty root leaf under the meta
page's exclusive lock; the insert then descends again and goes through the
normal checks, so two backends racing to insert the first key cannot both
skip the uniqueness check.

The split point is chosen as nbtree's nbtsplitloc.c chooses it
(bark_findsplitloc, barksplitloc.c), by bytes rather than item count: the
rightmost page on a level leaves its left half at the fillfactor (the leaf
fillfactor reloption, or BARK_NONLEAF_FILLFACTOR), so ascending inserts fill
pages the way CREATE INDEX does; other pages split evenly.  On a leaf the
choice among nearby split points prefers one between keys that differ early,
moves out of a run of equal keys when the page is not all one run, and leaves
the left page BARK_SINGLEVAL_FILLFACTOR full when the page holds a single key
that no later page holds.  Equal keys may sit on both sides of a split; the
high key between them then carries a heap TID (see "Heap TID order").

A split point falls between entries, and a LIST or POSTING entry can take a
third of the page, so on such a page the single-value split would leave the
left page with every entry that fits, nearly full.  When the incoming heap
TID sorts after every entry on it, as inserts in heap TID order do, the last
entry is cut instead (bark_singleval_cut, bark_entry_cut): its lower part
stays, sized so the left page ends at the fill factor, and its upper part,
with the new TID, goes to the right page.  The split's record logs both
whole.  nbtree reaches the same fill by capping the last posting list its
deduplication pass forms on such a page (_bt_singleval_fillfactor).  The
free space matters after VACUUM: rows inserted into the heap space it frees
get heap TIDs throughout each key's run, and go back to these pages; pages
left nearly full would mostly overflow and split evenly.

Suffix truncation
-----------------

A pivot (a page's high key, or the downlink to a page) keeps only as many
leading key attributes as it needs, as in nbtree (_bt_truncate).  When a leaf
splits between lastleft, the left page's last item, and firstright, the right
page's first item, the left page's new high key is firstright with every key
attribute after the first one on which the two differ removed: it keeps
1 + (the number of leading key attributes on which lastleft and firstright
are equal), up to all of them (bark_truncate_pivot, bark_keep_natts).  When
the two are equal on every key attribute, the pivot keeps them all and a heap
TID, firstright's lowest, as nbtree's does (BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID; see "Heap
TID order").  INCLUDE columns are never kept.  Equality is decided by the
opclass comparator, not by comparing stored images: two values can differ
in image yet compare equal, and what matters is how the pivot compares.  The
downlink inserted into the parent is a copy of the high key, so the parent
separates the two pages by the same tuple.  An internal page's items are pivots already, so its split
copies the right page's first item, with the attributes it has, as the new
high key.  CREATE INDEX does the same: each finished leaf's high key is
truncated against its last item, and a copy of that high key is the next
page's downlink (nbtsort.c's _bt_buildadd).  A single-column index never
truncates.

The removed attributes compare as minus infinity (bark_compare_itups orders a
pivot with fewer attributes before a key that agrees on the attributes it
has, which also makes a level's first downlink, with none, minus infinity).
So if T is firstright truncated, lastleft < T <= firstright, and T equals
firstright only when nothing was removed.  BARK's invariant, keys(left page)
<= high key <= keys(right page), still holds, and so does
keys(child i-1) <= downlink i <= keys(child i) for every downlink; with heap
TIDs, (key, TID) of every left entry < high key <= (key, lowest TID) of every
right entry.  The consequences:

  * A lower-bound descent (nextkey=false, scans, and the unique check)
    compares keys only, follows the last downlink less than its bound, and
    so never starts right of the first match.
  * An insert descends with its key and heap TID straight to the leaf the
    entry belongs on (see "Heap TID order").
  * A key that is greater than T but less than firstright (it shares T's
    attributes and sorts below firstright) now belongs to the right page, for
    inserts and searches alike.  No such key existed when the split was made,
    since lastleft and firstright were adjacent.
  * A lower bound equal to a pivot that has exactly the bound's columns and
    no heap TID sorts after the pivot (bark_compare_bound), as a forward
    scankey does in nbtree's _bt_compare: every entry left of the pivot is
    strictly less than it on those columns, so the descent goes right of
    it rather than reading a leaf that holds no match.  A pivot with more
    columns than the bound, or with a heap TID, may have entries equal to
    the bound on its left, and the bound still sorts before it.

The split-point choice (barksplitloc.c) prefers split points whose neighbors
differ on an earlier attribute, so the high key keeps fewer attributes.  As
in nbtree, it sizes the new high key as if nothing were truncated.

An OVERSIZED pivot stays OVERSIZED when one of the attributes it keeps is too
large to fit inline; its BarkOverflowRef.natts records the attributes kept.
A high key and the downlink copied from it share one overflow chain, as do an
internal page's high key and the item it is copied from, and nothing else
shares a pivot's chain.  The only pivot chain ever freed is that of the
downlink VACUUM removes when it deletes an empty leaf (bark_delete_empty_leaf):
the right sibling's, which shares its chain only with the deleted leaf's high
key, since it is never its internal page's first data item.  So the sharing
needs no reference count.

Reading an overflow chain
-------------------------

bark_fetch_oversized follows a chain's bark_next links, one page at a
time.  A chunk directory on the first page, listing the chain's other
blocks so that a read_stream could request them together (RethinkDB's
blob reference, RETHINKDB-REVIEW F3, backlog R4b), was built, measured
and not adopted, because it made no cold lookup faster.  Release builds,
4000 keys of 30 to 60 kB (4 to 8 pages each), 400 cold point lookups,
median of 3, on NVMe: with contiguous chains, as a build or ascending
inserts write them, it was 46% slower with io_method=worker (3115 against
2140 ms) and 25% with sync, since following the links already reads
sequentially and the kernel's readahead serves the later pages; with
chains on scattered recycled pages it was 1% to 7% slower, since each
lookup's 25-odd descent and comparison reads dominate; warm lookups and
inserts did not change (within 1%).  Skipping the stream for a
contiguous directory gave back the contiguous case but no win elsewhere.
The branch is kept as archive/bark/r4b-chunkdir, and the measurements are
in the BARK team's BENCHMARK-2026-10.md, "R4b (parked)".  Storage with a
much higher read latency than local NVMe might change the answer.

Heap TID order
--------------

Leaf entries are ordered by (key, heap TID), as nbtree's are since PG12
(version 4 of its format).  Every leaf entry covers a range of heap TIDs
(bark_entry_tid_range): a SINGLE its t_tid, an OVERSIZED entry its ref's
locator, a LIST its first to last member, a POSTING its sbm's minimum to
maximum.  Within a run of equal keys the ranges are disjoint and ascending,
across pages too.  Nothing else is ordered by TID: scans, VACUUM, the KNN
search, split-point choice and the build's grouping of runs compare keys
only (bark_compare_itups); only placement (the descent, the fast path and
the step right after a unique check) and amcheck's order checks use
bark_compare_itups_tid.

A pivot between two entries equal on every key attribute keeps the right
entry's lowest TID after its key data, as the last ItemPointerData of the
MAXALIGNed tuple, with BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID set in its status nibble (the bit
BARK_HAS_TAG has on a leaf entry, which a plain pivot never has); an
OVERSIZED pivot keeps it in BarkOverflowRef.pivottid.  A pivot without one
is minus infinity on the TID.  To leave room for it, a leaf entry is stored
inline only up to BarkMaxItemSize less the pivot suffix reserve
(BarkPivotSuffixReserve: 8 bytes, or 16 in an index built for version tags),
as nbtree's BTMaxItemSize does, and a split sizes a leaf's new high key with
it.  Entries equal on (key, TID), which only version tags make possible, are
ordered by tag; see "Version tags".

An insert descends with (key, TID), moving right past a high key whose
(key, TID) is <= its own (an equal TID belongs right, since the high key's
is the right page's lowest), and goes in front of the first entry that sorts
after it.  A unique check (nbtree's _bt_doinsert) descends on the key alone
to the first leaf that can hold it, since the live entry of the key may be
anywhere in the run; bark_check_unique scans right from there while keys
are equal; the insert then steps right, with lock coupling, to the leaf the
TID belongs on (bark_insert_stepright, _bt_stepright).  A key with a NULL
attribute skips the check unless the index is NULLS NOT DISTINCT; then it
is checked like any other key, since the comparison treats two NULLs in a
column as equal.  The build's duplicate check follows the same rule.

As in nbtree's _bt_search_insert, an insert that placed its entry on the
rightmost leaf of a tree whose root is at level 2 or higher caches that leaf
(RelationSetTargetBlock), or after splitting it the new right page.  The next
insert of a key that is not oversized takes the cached leaf instead of
descending if a conditional exclusive lock on it succeeds and it is still a
live rightmost leaf with no unfinished split, holding at least one entry,
with (key, TID) sorting after the first and room for the new entry
(bark_fastpath_leaf).  Such an insert has no stack and so must not split: an
insert whose TID falls inside an entry that cannot take it gives the leaf up
and descends.  A unique check, as in nbtree, makes the test on the key alone:
a key above the first entry's has no run on the left sibling, so the check
and the insert both stay on the cached leaf.  An insert that lands anywhere
else forgets the cached leaf.

Coalescing considers the entry just before the insert position, whose range
starts at or below the new TID.  A TID above its range is added to it (the
common case) or gets a SINGLE of its own.  A TID inside its range must go
into it.  When the entry cannot take it, the entry is divided around the TID
into two entries of the key (bark_entry_swap_tid): in the middle of its
members, not nbtree's posting-list swap of the highest member, because BARK
has no deduplication pass to merge the SINGLEs such swaps would leave.  The
two parts replace the entry in place (XLOG_BARK_INSERT_SWAP) when they fit,
else in a page split (bark_split's replaced entry, nbtree's origpostingoff).
A TID already in the entry is reported as corruption, as nbtree does.

A leaf insert keeps 8 bytes of a page's free space back (bark_leaf_free_space).
Coalescing fills a page a few bytes at a time, to the last byte; when such a
page splits inside a run, its new high key, which has a heap TID, is 8 bytes
larger than the one it replaces, and without the reserve the left half could
not keep all its entries: a LIST of up to a third of a page would move right
for want of 8 bytes, leaving that much free on the left for good.

Indexes built before heap TID order (version 1) are refused with a hint to
REINDEX them: the meta page's version is checked in bark_get_root, which
every descent reaches on a root-cache miss, and by bark_index_check.
Version 3 adds version tags (see "Version tags"); a version 2 index has
none, its pages are laid out as version 3 lays out an index on a heap
table, and it is read and written as it is.  Only a build (CREATE INDEX,
REINDEX, any rewrite) writes version 3.

Block 0 is always the meta page (BarkMetaPageData): a magic number
(BARK_MAGIC), the on-disk version (BARK_VERSION), the current root block and
its level, whether all key columns are "equalimage" (which governs
whether duplicate keys can be deduplicated losslessly), bark_flags and
bark_nkeys (see "Multikey keys").  bark_allequalimage is written by CREATE
INDEX and ambuildempty and shown by pageinspect's bark_metap; the access
method does not read it back: inserts and the build call
bark_allequalimage() instead (see "Entry encoding").  A descent does not
read the meta page: the root block and level are cached in rd_amcache
(BarkAmCache, which also caches the index's extracted column, whether it is
multikey and the markers seen), as nbtree caches its meta page, and the
cached page is used only if, under its lock, it is still a root (BARK_ROOT
set, not deleted or half-dead) at the cached level (bark_get_root_buffer).
A root split clears BARK_ROOT on the old root, so a stale cache entry fails
that check and the meta page is read instead.

Level-1 entry cache
-------------------

Every descent starting at the root writes the root's buffer header: its pin
count and its content lock's share count live in one state word.  On a
machine with more than one socket that word's cache line moves between
sockets on every lookup, and measured throughput falls past one socket's
worth of clients.  Re-pinning the root through ReadRecentBuffer made it
worse (its tag reads share the line); only not touching the root helps.

So the root cache also keeps a table of level-1 pages and their low keys
(BarkAmCache's level-1 slots), and every descent, read or write, starts at
the slot with the greatest low key it may start at (bark_l1_start), by the
rule bark_binsrch uses to pick a downlink: low key <= key for nextkey,
< key otherwise.  It locks that page, checks it is still a live level-1
internal page and that the key is within it (not past its high key, by
bark_moveright's rule), and goes on as if it had reached it through a
downlink: bark_moveright first, then down to the leaf.  It never reads the
root or the levels between.  When no slot qualifies, the page check fails,
or the key is past the page, the descent starts at the root as before, and
records the key's own page on the way.  The second check matters: the
table holds only pages descents have passed, so the nearest cached page
can be far left of the key's, and moving right across the pages between
would lock and pin each one, which costs more than the root (measured: 30
to 78 buffer hits a lookup, against 4).

Correctness rests on two facts.  BARK never deletes or merges an internal
page (VACUUM removes only empty interior leaves, see "Page deletion and
recycling"), so a level-1 page stays a level-1 page for the life of the
relfilenode.  And an internal page's low key never changes: a split keeps a
page's left part, so it moves only the high key left.  Every key the root
would route to a page P at or right of the slot's page S is >= S's low key,
every page between S and P has a high key <= that key, and so moving right
from S reaches P, as moving right does after a concurrent split.  A slot
may name a page that has split since; that costs move-rights, not a wrong
answer.

The table is filled as a side effect of ordinary descents: a level-2 page's
downlink is its child's low key, so each time a descent follows one (other
than the page's first, whose minus-infinity key is local to the page) it
records the pair (bark_l1_remember).  Slots are kept sorted; the table
lives in the same rd_amcache chunk as the markers, after them, so a
relcache rebuild (REINDEX, TRUNCATE, an invalidation) drops it with the
rest, and a new relfilenode starts empty.  It is capped at 2048 slots and
256 kB of keys per index.  At the cap it is thinned, not dropped: every other
slot is forgotten (bark_l1_thin), so the table keeps covering the whole key
range at half the density, and the descents that miss refill it.  A new
marker, which goes before the table in the chunk, moves the table up past it
rather than dropping it.

A write descent that starts at a cached level-1 page records a path that
begins there: the page's frame has no parent though the page is not the
root, and says so (BarkStackData.bark_cached).  Most inserts never need
more.  A leaf split puts its downlink into the level-1 page through that
frame as usual.  When the level-1 page splits too, and must put a downlink
into its parent, bark_stack_resolve_parent first finds the path above it
by a share-locked descent from the root with the page's high key, stopping
above its level (bark_find_upper_path), as page deletion finds a leaf's
parent.  It runs with the level-1 page and its child exclusive-locked, and
locks only pages above them, top down and one at a time, as any descent
from the root does, so it cannot deadlock with them; bark_getstackbuf then
moves right from the frame it gives if that page split since.  The cost is
one descent from the root per split that reaches past level 1, not one per
insert.  Finishing an incomplete split of the level-1 page (bark_moveright
before the page has a frame, or bark_getstackbuf) passes no path above it,
and takes bark_insert_parent's no-stack branch, which finds the parent from
the leftmost page of the level above; such splits are rare (an error or a
crash left them).

Tests: bark_l1_cache (every value, ranges across cached boundaries, missing
keys, backward scans, inserts that split cached pages, VACUUM page deletion,
a root split from level 2 to 3, REINDEX, TRUNCATE, a two-column index with
truncated pivots, DESC NULLS FIRST, and 2 kB keys that overflow the table's
256 kB cap), each compared with a sequential scan;
the bark_l1_concurrent isolation spec (another backend splitting cached
pages and the root, and a split while a descent from a cached page is
stopped before its leaf); the bark-l1-start injection point shows which
descents start below the root, and bark-l1-stale makes the page check fail
so that the fallback (drop the table, start at the root) is tested.

Measured on m7i.metal-48xl (2 sockets, 192 vCPU), prepared point lookups
on a 10M-row unique index whose root is at level 2: 0.99x at 1 client,
1.08x at 16-48, 1.42-1.58x at 96-768; the root's buffer header leaves the
top of perf c2c's table and remote HITMs fall a third.

A meta page written before bark_flags and bark_nkeys existed ends, by its
pd_lower, at bark_allequalimage, and PageInit zeroed the rest, so it reads 0
in both, which is right for an index with no extracted column.  The format
version stays 2.  A writer of either field first moves pd_lower past them
(bark_meta_cover), so the standard-layout image it logs keeps them.

TID stride
----------

Every mapping of a heap TID to an integer is laid out for the table's
largest locator offset, the index's TID stride: a POSTING entry's sbm key
(bark_tid_to_key, block * stride + offset - 1), a coded LIST's integer
(block << bits | offset, with max(11, bits of the stride) offset bits), and
the scan seen set's radix-tree key (bark_tid_to_key's).  For heap the
stride is MaxHeapTuplesPerPage, and a coded LIST's 11 bits are GIN's.  A
table AM may declare larger offsets (LocatorDesc.max_offset, at most
MaxOffsetNumber); the mappings then use its bound, so offsets from
different blocks never meet.

CREATE INDEX takes the stride from the table's LocatorDesc
(bark_heap_tid_stride) and stores it in the meta page's bark_tid_stride,
which sits in what was the padding after bark_allequalimage: the struct
keeps its size and pd_lower its place, so a heap index's meta page is the
one it was, byte for byte, but for that field, and every other page is
unchanged.  A meta page written before the field reads 0 there (PageInit
zeroed the padding), which bark_index_tid_stride reads as
MaxHeapTuplesPerPage, the only stride such an index can have.
bark_index_tid_stride caches the value in rd_amcache; it changes only with
the relfilenode (a rebuild: REINDEX, or the rewrite of ALTER TABLE ... SET
ACCESS METHOD, which writes the new table's stride), and a new relfilenode
drops rd_amcache.  BarkKeyInfo carries it to the code that forms and reads
entries.  A TID whose offset exceeds the stride cannot be mapped, so
CREATE INDEX's callback and aminsert refuse one as corruption
(bark_check_tid_offset): the table's locators changed without a rebuild.

An index scan through GIN's query interface keeps its result in a
TIDBitmap, which holds offsets only up to TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE (heap's
bound).  For a larger stride the scan folds heap block B's offset O into
bitmap page B * fold + (O - 1) / TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE
(bark_mk_gin_tbm_add), one-to-one and in TID order, and bark_mk_gin_load
turns each page back; work_mem bounds it as before.  The executor's bitmaps
are not folded: the planner makes no bitmap scan of a table whose offsets
exceed TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE (plancat.c).

Multikey keys
-------------

An index column whose operator class extracts several keys from one value
(procedure 7; "M1: multikey keys" in BARK-Design.mediawiki) is an extracted
column, and an index has at most one.  A row has one entry per distinct key
of that column, each with the row's other columns and heap TID; heap TID
order makes (key, TID) name one entry member.  bark_extract_row_keys runs
procedure 7 in a per-row memory context, sorts the keys with procedure 1 in
the column's index order and removes duplicates, as ginExtractEntries does.
A NULL value, a value with no keys and a key flagged NULL all become one
NULL key, so every row has an entry.

bark_insert_row_keys does this for an insert, in the per-row context of the
BarkInsertState (reset after each row), sets the multikey flag and inserts
the row's unseen markers (both below), forms one entry per key, and hands
them to bark_insert_entries, which inserts them in ascending (key, TID)
order in one pass.  CREATE INDEX extracts in its build callback
(bark_build_row_keys) and spools one tuple per key; keys too large for the
sort are inserted by the second pass (bark_insert_oversized_keys), which
runs procedure 7 again.  NULLS NOT DISTINCT on a unique index with an
extracted column, an exclusion constraint on one whose class has no
procedure 8, and two extracted columns are refused before anything is
built (bark_check_multikey_index).
The first descends as any insert does, or takes the cached
rightmost leaf; each later one goes on the leaf the previous one went on
when it belongs there (searched from that offset), else on the right
sibling, locked before the leaf is released, when it belongs there, else by
a new descent with no lock held.  A split is always followed by a new
descent.  Each entry goes through bark_insert_key, the code a single entry
always took (coalescing, bottom-up deletion, the single-value cut, merging,
the split), and its own WAL record, except that a run of the row's keys that go on
one leaf as plain new entries is logged as one INSERT_MULTI record.  A crash
between two entries of a row leaves entries of an uncommitted row, which
VACUUM removes.

A unique index with an extracted column is unique over its entries' key
columns ("M4" in BARK-Design.mediawiki): two rows conflict when they share
a key under equal fixed columns.  bark_insert_row_keys marks each key to
check in checkkeys: none when a fixed column is NULL, and never the NULL
key.  bark_insert_entries checks each marked key, in the row's ascending
order, under the exclusive lock of the first leaf that can hold it, and
inserts it before releasing that lock, nbtree's protocol for one key
applied per key.  For a later key the step from the previous key's leaf
uses the key-only move-right test (bark_belongs_on_leaf with no scantid),
then bark_insert_stepright moves to the key's heap TID place.  A conflict
with an in-progress row releases the leaf, waits, and descends again for
that key only; the keys before it stay in.  Two inserters cannot wait on
each other in a cycle, since both insert in ascending key order; a wait on
an in-progress deleter can deadlock as in nbtree, and the deadlock detector
ends one.  A committed conflict raises the unique violation with the
row's values as bark_insert received them (never the stored key, which is
of the storage type) and the shared key printed with the storage type's
output function; the keys already inserted are entries of the failed row,
which VACUUM removes.  UNIQUE_CHECK_PARTIAL neither waits nor errors: the
result becomes false at the first conflict and the remaining keys are
inserted unchecked.  UNIQUE_CHECK_EXISTING checks every key and inserts
none.  CREATE INDEX puts the keys of a row that is not alive in the second
sort, which bark_load does not check, and passes the oversized pass the
row's check; bark_load's detail is "Duplicate keys exist." for such an
index.  get_relation_info sets info->unique false for it, since a row may
hold many keys.  Such an index needs the class's procedure 15
(translate-cmptype, registered under ("any", "any") as GiST's 12 is),
which bark_translate_cmptype asks for COMPARE_OVERLAP only: two rows
conflict when their values share a key, which is overlap, not equality.
bark_check_multikey_index refuses a unique index whose class names no
overlap operator under its input type.  INSERT ... ON CONFLICT's
pre-check (BuildSpeculativeIndexInfo, which falls back to COMPARE_OVERLAP
when a column has no equality) scans with that operator, and the seen set
returns each row once, so check_exclusion_or_unique_constraint never sees
its own row twice.  A foreign key (transformFkeyCheckAttrs), REFRESH
MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY (is_usable_unique_index) and REPLICA
IDENTITY (ATExecReplicaIdentity) need equality on every column, so the
first two pass such an index by and the third refuses it.

Exclusion constraints.  An exclusion constraint on an extracted column may
use any operator core accepts for one: commutative, and in the column's
operator family with a strategy (ComputeIndexAttrs).  BARK adds no rule of
its own about the operator.  The executor checks the constraint by scanning
the index for the new row's value under the operator with a dirty snapshot,
skipping the row's own TID, and calling the operator on the values of the
rows the scan returns with xs_recheck
(check_exclusion_or_unique_constraint).  So the scan must return every
conflicting row, once; a row that does not conflict may come back with
xs_recheck, and the recheck drops it.  That is what a WHERE clause on the
operator already needs of procedure 8.  For array overlap, as the test
class bark_int4_array_ops scans it, the boundaries are the value's distinct
non-NULL keys, exact: a row that shares one has an entry there.  The NULL
key, which stands for a NULL value, an empty value and a NULL element, is
in no overlap boundary, so a row whose only key it is conflicts with
nothing, and a NULL element beside keys is ignored, as overlap ignores it.
For array = the points are rechecked, and a value with no key reads the
NULL entries, also rechecked.  A NULL entry is only ever read with
xs_recheck, so the operator tells {} from {NULL}, and the recheck drops a
row whose value is NULL (index_recheck_constraint takes the operator to be
strict, as the check does for a new NULL value, which it passes without a
scan).  A scan with a dirty snapshot always keeps the seen set
(bark_mk_rescan), so a row that matches through several entries comes back
once, at its first; that is needed, not only cheaper: the new row matches
itself through every key it shares, and the check raises "found self tuple
multiple times" for its own TID seen twice.  The row's own entries all have
its TID, so the check skips them as one.  An insert puts in all of a row's
entries before the check runs, so of two concurrent inserters of
conflicting rows the later to scan meets the other's entry and waits for
it, as with any index.  That also covers the seen set's one blind spot
under a dirty snapshot: once a dead row's TID is in the set, VACUUM may
remove the row's entries the scan has not reached yet and the heap give the
TID to a new row, whose entries the set then drops.  That row's entries
went in after this row's, so its own check meets this row.  The class must
scan by procedure 8: a GIN-interface scan requires an MVCC snapshot, so
bark_check_multikey_index refuses such a column, and one with neither
procedure, in a constraint.  The same check runs for each row when the
index is built (IndexCheckExclusion: ADD CONSTRAINT, REINDEX), at commit
for a deferrable constraint (unique_key_recheck), and as ON CONFLICT DO
NOTHING's pre-check; ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE refuses an exclusion constraint
in core.  The index stores no version tags (bark_indexcanversion), as no
multikey index does: an update that keeps the row's locator (TU_Affected)
leaves its entries, and core checks the constraint after the update as
after a HOT one (below).

The meta page's BARK_META_MULTIKEY bit says the index has held a row with
two or more entries.  An insert sets it before the first entry of such a
row, with no other page locked, logging the meta page whole
(log_newpage_buffer: once in an index's life, so no record type of its
own); CREATE INDEX sets it when any row had two keys.  It is never cleared.
bark_nkeys is the number of (key, TID) members, markers not counted, as of
the last CREATE INDEX or VACUUM, which logs the meta page whole when the
count changed.

A marker is a key procedure 7 returns through its fourth and fifth
arguments, a fact about the index rather than a row.  It is stored once, as
a SINGLE entry whose heap TID is (InvalidBlockNumber, 1): no heap block has
that number, and it sorts after every real TID of the key.  Its other key
columns are NULL.  An insert adds the row's markers that its backend has not
seen, before the row's keys, never coalesced and not again when present;
CREATE INDEX writes each distinct one once (each participant spools it with
the TID (InvalidBlockNumber, 2 + ParallelWorkerNumber), which for the
leader, whose number is -1, is the reserved TID, since tuplesort refuses
equal TIDs; the load writes the reserved TID).  Markers are never
removed.  Every reader of heap TIDs skips them: scans, KNN, VACUUM's
callback and its count, bottom-up deletion, merging and coalescing.
bark_index_has_marker looks one up.

The planner treats such an index as ordered only by its columns before the
extracted one: plancat leaves the sort family InvalidOid at a column whose
operator family is not btree's, and build_index_pathkeys stops there.

A column whose class has GIN's query interface (procedures 10 and 11 or
13, and 12 for partial match) and no procedure 8 (bark_index_gin_query;
"As built (M2a)" and "As built (M2c)" in the design document) is answered
row by row from all of a row's entries, so a scan computes its whole
result, in TID order, before its first row (bark_mk_gin_items), into a
TIDBitmap.  A bitmap scan's is the executor's; an index scan
(bark_mk_gin_gettuple) makes its own in ginCxt, bounded by work_mem alike,
and returns it a row at a time, forward or backward, with mark and restore
as a place in it (a page in iteration order and an offset on it).  Past
work_mem the bitmap goes lossy, and the index scan returns every offset of
a lossy heap page with a recheck: a fetch finds nothing at an offset with
no tuple, or with a heap-only tuple (found from its chain's root), so each
row comes back once and the recheck decides it.  A TIDBitmap iterates
forward only, so a backward step across a page or a restore walks a new
iteration to the page; the planner never asks a backward scan of such an
index.  The TIDs are collected with no leaf pinned,
so an index scan needs an MVCC snapshot (nbtree's dropPin argument: a slot
VACUUM frees and the heap reuses holds a row the snapshot cannot see), and
refuses any other.  It has no leaf position to mark dead entries on, so
kill_prior_tuple is ignored (its reads still skip the entries other scans
marked; see "Dead-entry hints and simple deletion"), and the seen set is
off (a row's TID is in
several boundaries' streams, merged into one).  bark_property answers
index_scan false only when the column is not the index's first: rows come
back in TID order, not in the order of the columns before it, which the
planner would assume (pathkeys, merge joins, KNN); get_relation_info then
clears amhasgettuple.  barkcostestimate makes the whole index cost startup
cost.  The scan calls extractQuery, turns its keys into point boundaries (a
partial-match key into a range filtered and ended by comparePartial), and
reads each boundary into a stream (BarkGinStream), iterated a heap page at
a time.  A point on the leading column comes in TID order (H1), so its
stream is exact: its TIDs and recheck flags in a list of chunks
(BarkGinChunk, 7.9 kB each, below AllocSet's chunk limit), taken from a
free list the scan keeps (ginfree, in ginChunkCxt), so a query reuses them
key after key and the scan rescan after rescan.  Any other boundary (a
partial-match range, the NULL entries, every key for ALL, a point on a
later column) is read into a TIDBitmap of its own, bounded by work_mem and
lossy beyond it, as ginget.c's collectMatchBitmap reads a partial match:
a bitmap needs no sort and absorbs a row's repeats within the boundary.  A
point whose TIDs stop ascending (key columns after the GIN-interface one,
whose order breaks a point's run) becomes such a bitmap from there on
(bark_gin_stream_to_bitmap).  The index scan's folded TIDs (a TID stride
above TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE) go to bitmaps only, since a stream's page
holds at most TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE offsets.  bark_mk_gin_key merges the
streams a heap page at a time with a binary heap on their page numbers, as
ginget.c's keyGetItem merges its entries' streams, and calls
triConsistent, or consistent, once per row on the per-key presence
vector, adding the row with its recheck flag.  A stream that holds the
page lossy is MAYBE in that vector; as in keyGetItem, the page is first
tried whole with the lossy streams MAYBE and the others FALSE, and goes in
lossy when that can match, else each exact offset on it is decided.
Without triConsistent the MAYBE vectors go through consistent as
ginlogic.c's shim does: every combination of up to four MAYBE entries,
MAYBE beyond.  With triConsistent, the streams are tried rarest first (by
the TIDs read into each) until a vector with them FALSE and the rest MAYBE
gives FALSE (startScanKey's required entries); only those feed the heap,
and the others are moved forward to each page it gives
(bark_gin_stream_seek), so a rare key with a frequent one costs about as
many consistent calls as the rare key has rows.  Every boundary is still
read to its end into its bitmap, so the frequent key's leaf pages are read
even when few of its rows are needed.

BARK allocates nothing above 8 kB for these streams.  Before, each stream
was an exact array of 12 bytes per TID, allocated huge and doubled, and a
scan with several keys merged their results into further such arrays: 134
MB for one stream over 10M rows, and a partial match over a path could
hold every TID of the column.  Now an exact stream is chunks of 7.9 kB
(9 bytes per TID), and every other stream, and each key's result when
there are several keys, is a TIDBitmap: exact, it costs about 64 bytes per
heap page it holds; past work_mem it goes lossy, a page per bit run, and
the heap recheck decides those rows.  The TIDBitmaps are core's, and their
pagetables are core's large allocations (a doubling simplehash from 128
entries), so a stream kept as a bitmap costs what a bitmap scan's own
bitmap costs; that is why a point, the common case (an AND of array
elements), keeps an exact stream: two point streams as bitmaps made a
2-key AND over 17k-row keys 41% slower than the old arrays, and the
chunks make it faster than either.  The streams of one key are freed
before the next key is read (each key runs in a context reset after it,
and an exact stream's chunks go back to the free list), so at most the
streams of one key plus one bitmap per key on the column are live.
Several keys on the column are intersected with tbm_intersect, the
elements of a ScalarArrayOp unioned in one bitmap, and the result copied
into the executor's bitmap (or the index scan's gintbm).  Apart from the
bitmaps, which are core's, the scan allocates nothing above 8 kB that it
did not before: the per-stream state (BarkGinStream, 1.1 kB), the chunks,
the per-page vectors and the batch.  No stream holds a leaf position: each
boundary is read with the scan's one position, in turn, with no pin kept
between boundaries, so the reads follow the ordinary scan rules ("Scans")
and a concurrent split or VACUUM between two boundaries' reads changes
nothing they have read (bark_multikey_gin_concurrent stops a scan between
two boundaries while another session splits leaves, deletes and
vacuums).

A bitmap scan whose key is the union of its boundaries skips the streams
(bark_mk_gin_union decides it): when consistent accepts every non-empty
presence vector of at most four query keys, every row of any boundary
matches, so bark_mk_gin_read_bitmap adds each boundary's TIDs straight to
the TIDBitmap, page by page, as ginget.c's collectMatchBitmap reads a
partial match.  A key whose rows alone need a recheck adds them with one,
and so does every key of a vector that needs a recheck when none of its
keys has one already.  That is the common shape of a partial match over a
whole path (DocumentDB's $regex, $size, $exists): there is no stream, no
sort and no merge, and memory is the bitmap's, bounded by work_mem and
lossy beyond it.  Each key on the column (or each element of its
ScalarArrayOp, whose union the key's bitmap is) can take this path; with
several keys each key fills a bitmap of its own, intersected with the
others'.

Bounded streams, next step.  Two things still grow with the data, and
both are left for later, with the shape of the work known.  First, every
stream is still its boundary read to its end before the merge starts, so a
rare key ANDed with a frequent one reads all of the frequent key's leaves
(GIN reads only the posting-tree pages the rare key's TIDs fall on: 0.10 ms
against BARK's 50 ms for @> with a rare and a half-the-rows element over
10M rows).  The remedy is a positioned stream per boundary: its own leaf
position (a BarkScanPosData of 6.5 kB and a page-sized copy of the leaf's
LIST and POSTING bodies), refilled a leaf at a time by bark_steppage with
no lock held across other streams' reads, and for an additional stream a
re-descent by (key, TID) to the next candidate, which BARK's heap TID
order makes possible.  A TID-ordered boundary needs only that.  A boundary
that is not TID-ordered across leaves (a partial match over many keys, the
NULL or ALL entries, a later column) needs either a TIDBitmap, as now, or
a bounded TID set of BARK's own built from chunks below 8 kB, lossy past a
budget, since core's TIDBitmap pagetable is one doubling allocation.
About 600 lines with the tests, in barkscan.c's GIN-interface block and
bark_readpage; not started.  Second, a multikey scan's page read decodes
each entry's members into so->entryTids and copies the survivors into the
tuple workspace (bark_save_members), which grows to the page's decoded
size: three allocations of 32 kB per query on a page of LISTs of 132 TIDs,
up to 166 kB with POSTING entries.  A GIN-interface read has no per-TID
filter (bark_mk_accept decides per entry and the seen set is off), so it
could keep each body coded, as a plain scan does, and decode a member run
at a time; the tuple workspace would then stay at BLCKSZ.

A read of an open-ended partial-match boundary pays procedure 12 on every
entry, and used to pay the column comparator against the boundary's start
twice more (bark_mk_filter, and bark_req_advance when procedure 12 refuses
the entry).  On the leading column, ascending, the read goes forward from
the start in key order with the NULLs at one end, so once one non-NULL key
is inside the boundary every later one is: bark_mk_gin_first sets
startlatch for such a read, and bark_mk_cmp, after the first key it finds
inside, answers "inside" without comparing (instart).  A later column
restarts its keys at each value of the columns before, and a DESC column's
lower side is its end, so neither latches.  The end of the range is still
procedure 12's to say.

Scans of an index with an extracted column ("Scans" under "M1: multikey
keys" in the design document) keep the btree-style key code away from the
column's keys, whose operators are not btree comparisons:
bark_mk_partition_keys moves them after so->numberOfKeys, the count every
other key loop uses, and only BarkMkScanState reads them.  bark_mk_setup
calls procedure 8 on the first of them (once per element of a
ScalarArrayOp), with BarkQueryFlags as a seventh argument when the function
takes one, and turns the boundaries into BarkMkBoundary ranges in the
column's index order (a DESC column swaps the sides), sorted.  Two
boundaries of one call that intersect are an error; those of different
SAOP elements are merged, and a merged range returns its rows with
xs_recheck instead of running procedure 9.  searchnulls, IS NULL and IS NOT
NULL add a range of the NULL entries, whose rows also come back with
xs_recheck, since the NULL key may be a NULL value, an empty one or a NULL
element.  Further keys on the column only make every row come back with
xs_recheck: a row may satisfy two of them through two keys.

When every column before the extracted one has an equality (or an
equality array), the ranges are one more required key: a BarkArrayKeyState
whose mk field points at them, the last digit of the required-key odometer.
bark_req_cmp, bark_req_seek and bark_req_advance compare an entry with the
current range as they compare it with an element, bark_mk_bound puts the
range's start (its end, backward; a NULL bound column for the NULL range,
which bark_compare_bound places by the column's NULLS option) into the
descent bound, and the scan re-descends where the next range starts past
the page, as for the next element.  bark_req_past_range reads the column
after the extracted one only inside a point range.  Otherwise the ranges
filter: bark_tuple_matches ends with bark_mk_filter, a binary search over
them.  Skip scan is off when the extracted column is column 1 and has keys.

An entry that passes goes to bark_mk_accept (procedure 9 when its range
asked for it: false drops the entry, maybe sets the item's recheck flag)
and then, in bark_readpage, to bark_mk_seen, the seen set: a radix tree
(lib/radixtree.h, local, in its own context) from bark_tid_to_key(tid) >> 6
(at the index's TID stride) to a 64-bit word with one bit per TID of that
window.  A TID already in it
is dropped; a new one is set and kept.  So a page's items are free of
repeats when the read copies them, and gettuple returns them as they are.
Entries rejected by a filter or procedure 9 never reach the set.  With
fixed columns before the extracted one, bark_mk_group empties the set when
the entry's values there differ from those of the rows in it: all of a
row's entries share them.  bark_mk_rescan decides whether the set is used:
not for a scan with exactly one range that is a point, nor for one whose
first key (not a ScalarArrayOp) procedure 8 answered with
BarkQueryFlags.oneentry, nor when the meta
page's BARK_META_MULTIKEY is clear (read by bark_index_is_multikey after the
scan's snapshot was taken; a cached true is never stale), but always under
a non-MVCC snapshot, whose rows' inserts may still be running.  BARK sets
BarkQueryFlags.index to the scanned index before calling procedure 8, so a
class can read its markers there (bark_index_has_marker); procedure 8 runs
at every rescan, so its answer is the index's after the scan's snapshot,
whatever the plan was made from.  The markers are read as the multikey flag
is: a row visible to the snapshot inserted its markers before its entries.  A bitmap
scan never uses it (TIDBitmap absorbs repeats) and passes the items'
recheck flags to tbm_add_tuples.  The set grows past get_hash_memory_limit()
rather than return a row twice; it holds at most one group's rows.

Mark and restore: bark_markpos starts an undo list; every TID added to the
set while marked is appended to it, and the set is not emptied for a new
group.  bark_restrpos clears those TIDs from the set and empties the list,
so the rows after the mark come back; a restore with no position (the mark
was taken before the first row) empties the set.

A scan that changed direction with the set in use would walk back over
entries whose rows the set drops, so an index with an extracted column
has no backward scans: bark_property answers backward_scan false for it,
from its operator classes, and the executor's IndexSupportsBackwardScan
asks amproperty before amcanbackward, so a scroll cursor over such an
index gets a Material node.  The scan still raises an error if a caller
that did not ask changes direction with the set in use.  A cursor not
declared SCROLL is then not made scrollable (portalcmds.c makes it so only
when the plan runs backward without a Material node).  plancat gives the
index no parallel scans (amcanparallel false), since the workers read
different leaves and could not share the set; a direct caller's parallel
scan that needs the set still gets an error.  A parallel bitmap heap scan
is fine.  An ordering key on the extracted column raises an error (not
served yet).  bark_property also reports orderable, asc, desc, nulls_first
and nulls_last false for the extracted column and every key column after
it, which form no pathkeys, and distance_orderable true for a leading
column whose family has an ordering operator its sort family can sort
(the KNN classes), false otherwise; INCLUDE columns and the other
properties get the generic answers.  A KNN scan on a fixed leading column
runs every entry through bark_mk_accept and bark_mk_seen, without group
clears, since its two cursors interleave groups.

Index-only scans: bark_canreturn answers false for the extracted column,
so the planner reads only the other columns.  A key does not determine its
row's value: a row with several keys has an entry per key, and the single
NULL key stands for a NULL value, an empty value and a NULL element alike,
so any value rebuilt from one entry could be wrong.  Procedure 14 is
therefore not called.  The scan returns xs_hitup, formed by
bark_mk_form_hitup from the entry, with the extracted column NULL and
name_ops' cstring columns made names again, in hitupdesc, the index's
descriptor with the column's input type.


Implementing btree
------------------

barkhandler sets amimplements = btree, so BARK's code can store and
search an index created USING btree (pg_index.indimplam; "Index
Implementations" in indexam.sgml), chosen by default_btree_implementation
or a table AM's index_implementation callback.  The relcache takes
rd_indam from BARK's handler, so every callback and capability flag of
such an index is BARK's, and barkoptions parses its reloptions; but its
relam is btree, its operator classes are btree's, and everything about
btree semantics (planner, opclass commands, pg_get_indexdef, strategy
translation) goes through btree.

Nothing in BARK tests relam, so only the operator families matter.  A
btree class's family is btree's, so bark_opfamily_is_own is false for
every column, which takes BARK's scalar path throughout: no column
extracts keys, no procedure past 6 is read (index_getprocid returns
InvalidOid for 7-15, since a btree family has none), and
bark_translate_cmptype's procedure 15 is never consulted.  The planner
and executor translate strategies with the logical AM's routine
(IndexAmTranslate* take relam or the family's opfmethod), and amvalidate
and amadjustmembers are called for the class's own AM, never BARK's for a
btree class.  Core accepts BARK's amsupport of 15 (more than btree's 6;
the relcache sizes the support arrays from it) and amstrategies of 0
(no core code reads an index's amstrategies).

The <~> ordering operators in pg_amop.dat sit in btree's integer_ops
with amopmethod bark, so a BARK-implemented int2/int4/int8 btree index
has distance-ordered paths and an nbtree one has none: its amcanorderbyop
is nbtree's false.  That is the intended difference, a capability of the
implementation.

btree's reloptions deduplicate_items and vacuum_cleanup_index_scale_factor
are registered for RELOPT_KIND_BARK as well.  deduplicate_items = off makes
the writers (bark_index_coalesces) store SINGLEs only, as for a unique
index; the scale factor is ignored.

REPACK's sort path tests relam == btree and sorts with
tuplesort_begin_cluster, whose _bt_mkscankey(rel, NULL) reads only the
relcache's opclass data, so it is right for a BARK-implemented index.  The
RI fast path tests relam too and probes through the index AM API.
bt_index_check, pageinspect's bt_* functions and pgstatindex refuse the
index (they compare the implementation, IndexIsNbtree); bark_index_check
accepts it, since index_checkable compares the index's handler
(rd_amhandler, the implementation's) with barkhandler, as for an alias
access method.  pg_amcheck checks such an index as a BARK index, with
bark_index_check or bark_index_parent_check (it reads pg_index.indimplam),
given amcheck 1.7; it skips a btree index any other access method
implements, which bt_index_check would refuse.


HOT updates
-----------

barkcomparedatums is BARK's amcomparedatums.  During UPDATE, for a column a
BARK index uses whose value changed, the executor asks whether the new
value gives the index exactly the entries the old one gave it; if every
index using the column says yes, the update may be HOT and no index gets
an entry for the new version.  So yes must mean that no scan, unique
check, VACUUM pass or amcheck probe could tell the old entries from those
an insert of the new value would make.  BARK answers no for an index that
is not ready or not valid.

Core does not ask about a column an index-only scan can return
(bark_canreturn): the index stores the value itself, so core decides by
image, and treats a value stored compressed or out of line and the same
value stored plain as unchanged for every index.  That leaves the
extracted column, and the other columns, scalar keys and INCLUDE columns,
of an index whose extracted column uses GIN's query interface, which
returns no column.  Those are stored as given, so yes means the same bytes
once detoasted (datum_image_eq): numeric 1.0 and 1.00 differ.  The
extracted column of an exclusion constraint's index scans by procedure 8
(bark_check_multikey_index), so the index's other columns can be returned
and core compares them itself.  Core asks about the extracted column only
for a constraint checked immediately (IndexDatumsDiffer): a deferred check
takes the value the entries were made from to be every version's.

For the extracted column both values go through bark_extract_row_keys, as
an insert's do, and yes means the same number of keys, NULL in the same
places, each pair equal by procedure 1 and as images (the entry stores the
key's bytes, which procedures 9 and 12 read), and every marker of the new
value among the old value's.
Markers are inserted once and never removed, so a marker only the new
value has is an insert the index needs; one only the old value has stays
either way.  {NULL} and {} both give the single NULL key, so their entries
are the same bytes, and an update between them is HOT; a scan that has to
tell them apart rechecks the row anyway.

The image test is dropped for one kind of index, one whose extracted
column uses GIN's query interface, is not equalimage, and coalesces
(BarkInsertState.ginrule, bark_index_may_coalesce): there an entry keeps
the image of the first row that formed it and later rows of an equal key
add only their TIDs, so a row's own image need not be in the index
whatever the update does.  Nothing returns the key as a row's value, and
the class only compares it with procedure 1, as gincomparedatums relies on
for GIN.  heapallindexed refuses such an index for the same reason.  A
unique index and an index with INCLUDE columns do not coalesce, and
compare images.

A unique index needs nothing more.  Its old entries keep their keys and
reach the live version through the HOT chain, so the next insert of an
equal key meets them and its check (table_fetch_tid follows the chain)
finds the conflict.  Core does not ask about the columns of a unique index
without a predicate (they can be referenced as keys, and core decides
them by value), so in practice this matters for partial unique indexes.

Nor does an exclusion constraint's index, whatever its operator.  Values
with the same keys need not have the same conflicts: under array =,
{1,2} and {2,1} have one key set and are not equal, and {NULL} and {} have
the single NULL key.  But yes is an answer about the entries.  When it
lets an update keep them although the value changed, core checks the
constraint for the new value after the update (ExecCheckHotExclusion), as
inserting an entry would have; a concurrent inserter reaches the new
version through the old entries.  Under overlap, which reads only the
keys, that check finds what the old value's would have.

The call reuses the statement's BarkInsertState from ii_AmCache, and the
keys are allocated in the caller's per-row context.  Core's cassert
cross-check covers expression proofs, not an access method's answer, so
under USE_ASSERT_CHECKING a yes is checked by bark_entries_same, which
forms both values' leaf entries as bark_insert_row_keys would and compares
their bytes (by procedure 1 for a GIN-rule column), and raises an error
when they differ.


Operator classes and validation
-------------------------------

BARK declares ambtreeopfamilies: a BARK operator class for a scalar type
is a member of the btree operator family of its type, not of a family of
its own.  pg_opclass has one bark row per btree row (45), with btree's name,
family, input type, storage type and default, and pg_amop and pg_amproc
have nothing for BARK beyond the KNN ordering operators: BARK uses the
family's btree operators and support functions.  opclasscmds.c lets
CREATE OPERATOR CLASS ... USING bark name a btree family (it looks in the
AM's own families first, then btree's).  The planner treats such a column
as it treats a btree column (pathkeys, row comparisons, equivalence
classes); bark_translate_strategy and bark_translate_cmptype are btree's
mapping.

Only support function 1 is required; BARK calls it (bark_build_keyinfo) to
order entries, and the family's cross-type ORDER procs to position scans on
constants of other types (bark_setup_key_procs).  tuplesort reads 1 and 2
for the build (bark_sort_begin, through the shared family), 4
decides coalescing (bark_allequalimage), and 6 drives the skip scan's next
value (PrepareSkipSupportFromOpclass).  BARK never calls in_range (3).

The KNN ordering operators <~> (int2, int4, int8; pg_operator OIDs
9473-9475) are amop rows in btree's integer_ops family with amopmethod
bark, strategy 6 (BARK_KNN_STRATEGY), purpose 'o' and sort family
float_ops.  btree does not use them; opr_sanity's check that an amop row's AM is its
family's exempts btree's families.
The distance functions are bark_int2/4/8_distance in barkutils.c.

A multikey class lives in an operator family whose opfmethod is bark
("Multikey keys"; "M1: multikey keys" in the design document).  For BARK's
own families the handler declares amstrategies 0 (any strategy number),
amsupport 15 (BARK_MULTIKEY_NPROCS) and amstorage true, so the core code
accepts any strategy, support functions up to 15 and a STORAGE clause.
barkadjustmembers keeps btree families as strict as before: in a btree
family it refuses strategies past 5, support functions past 6 and a
storage type, any of which would make btvalidate report btree's own
classes in the family invalid.  bark_translate_strategy and
bark_translate_cmptype return COMPARE_INVALID and InvalidStrategy for a
BARK family (except COMPARE_OVERLAP through procedure 15, above), so the planner never reads = or < semantics into a multikey
class's operators, and plancat gives such a column InvalidOid as its sort
family, where build_index_pathkeys stops.

A family is BARK's own when its access method is not btree
(bark_opfamily_is_own), not when it is BARK_AM_OID: CREATE ACCESS METHOD x
TYPE INDEX HANDLER barkhandler runs this code under another OID, and its
families must be read as BARK's (an alias's multikey family read as a btree
family would have its strategies taken for btree's and its keys never
extracted).  The test is sound because the callers pass only a family of a
BARK index or class, and with ambtreeopfamilies the only foreign families
such a class can name are btree's.  Likewise code that reads a relation as
a BARK index (pageinspect, pgstattuple, the reloption accessors, the
multikey test modules) asks bark_relation_is_bark, which tests the
relation's handler, and amcheck's index_checkable compares the index's
handler with the expected access method's.  pg_amcheck selects indexes by
access method OID in SQL and so does not check an alias's indexes.

CREATE INDEX refuses a column whose class is in a family of BARK's own but
has no procedure 7 (bark_check_multikey_index).  Such a class is not
multikey, so the scan would read its operators' strategy numbers as btree's
(bark_key_bounds), but a BARK family allows any number: an operator <
registered as strategy 4 would position and stop the scan as >=, and the
scan would return wrong rows.  amvalidate reports the class invalid too.

barkvalidate checks a class in a btree family as btvalidate would for the
procedures BARK uses: support functions 1-6 with btree's signatures, the
class's own-type comparator present, search operators at strategies 1-5
returning bool, and ordering operators at strategy 6 with a sort family.
A class in a BARK family goes to bark_validate_multikey, which checks
procedures 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6 with btree's signatures over the storage type,
7-9 with pg_extended_btree's (7 with two to five arguments, 8 with six or
seven), 10-13 with GIN's and 14 as value (key); requires 1 and 7, and 9
with 8; refuses in_range (3); and accepts any strategy number.  Support
functions of a multikey class are registered under its input type, as
GIN's are, and the report for a comparator registered under the storage
type says so.  A class in a BARK family without procedure 7 is not
extracted (bark_opfamily_extracts) and is used as a scalar class, but
amvalidate reports it invalid.

CREATE INDEX
------------

bark_build (barksort.c) scans the heap through table_index_build_scan into a
tuplesort ordered by (key, heap TID, tag), then writes the tree bottom-up, as
nbtsort.c does; keys too large for the sort go in afterwards, by a second
heap scan and the normal insert path.  A parallel build lets workers scan
and sort; only the leader writes pages.

The sort is BARK's own tuplesort variant (bark_sort_begin), on the hooks
tuplesort.h exports for variants: each key column's sort support comes from
the index's operator family, as tuplesort_begin_index_btree sets it up, and
key ties break on (heap TID, tag), read from a tagged SINGLE's trailer.
nbtree's sort breaks ties on t_tid, which a tagged SINGLE uses for its
trailer offset and status bits, so it cannot sort tagged entries.  The
build forms a row the table AM tagged as a tagged SINGLE before sorting it,
and bark_load writes it as it is, packed to the fill factor like any other
entry (see "Version tags").  At 1M rows of distinct int4 keys on
heap_versioned, release build, a build that tags every row takes 1.17x the
time of one that tags none, serial, and its index is 1.8x the size (4922
pages against 2737): a tagged SINGLE is 32 bytes, an untagged one 16.
Inserting tagged rows after the load, as the build did before, took 5.8x
the time and 5425 pages ("As built" under "Versioned locators" in
BARK-Design.mediawiki).

The build indexes every row the table AM passes to its callback.  The table
AM passes some with tupleIsAlive false: RECENTLY_DEAD rows, and rows deleted
by a transaction still in progress (heapam_index_build_range_scan).  Such a
row is no longer live, but a snapshot older than the build may still see it
and may scan the new index, so it must be indexed; the flag says only that
the row must not take part in the uniqueness check.  A truly dead row is
never passed.  In a unique index those rows are sorted apart, and bark_load
merges them into the live rows' order without checking them, as nbtsort.c's
_bt_load merges spool2; the oversized pass inserts them with
UNIQUE_CHECK_NO.  The fact is transient: it lives only in the build's sorts
and is never recorded on a page (no entry bit).  Each
entry is the same entry insert would have made for the row.  The build's
index_tuples count includes them, as nbtree's does; the table AM's
reltuples does not.

A HOT chain whose older members a snapshot still sees, but whose live member
has another key, cannot be indexed for that snapshot: the entry points at
the chain's root and leads to the live member.  The table AM leaves such
members out and sets IndexInfo.ii_BrokenHotChain, and index.c then marks
the new index indcheckxmin, so that older snapshots do not use it.  In a
parallel build each participant, the leader included, scans with its own
IndexInfo; a participant that saw a broken chain says so in the shared
state, and the leader copies it into the build's IndexInfo before index.c
looks, as nbtsort.c's _bt_parallel_heapscan does.

VACUUM
------

barkbulkdelete reads the index in physical block order, as btbulkdelete
does, and deletes the entries whose heap TIDs are dead.  Only live leaf pages
are cleaned; deleted pages, internal pages and overflow pages are skipped.
The blocks come from a read stream over [1, length), as btvacuumscan's do
since nbtree's c5c239e26e3: bark_vacuum_scan re-arms it for the blocks added
since each pass, and bark_vacuum_page gets each block's buffer from it.  A
page reached by backtracking (below) is off the stream's order and is read
directly.  Measured cold (BARK-Design.mediawiki, "As built (RethinkDB
review)"), it cut VACUUM's read calls sevenfold and left its time as it
was.

A leaf split during the scan can move entries from a page the scan has not
reached yet to a right page at a block it has already passed (a split takes
its right page from the free space map, which can hand out any block).  To
catch that, BARK uses vacuum cycle IDs, as nbtree does, from a registry of
its own (barkvacuum.c: bark_start_vacuum, bark_vacuum_cycleid,
bark_end_vacuum), so that BARK uses no nbtree internals.  It lives in shared
memory (BarkShmemCallbacks) under BarkVacuumLock: one slot per backend,
indexed by ProcNumber, holding the index's LockRelId and the cycle ID of the
VACUUM that backend runs on it, where nbtree's btvacinfo keeps a dense
array.  Starting and ending a VACUUM is a store to the backend's own slot; a
split's lookup reads all of them.  A leaf split stamps
both halves with the cycle ID of the VACUUM running on the index, if any,
while it holds both pages locked.  When VACUUM cleans a page that carries its
cycle ID and whose right sibling is at a lower block than the scan position,
it follows the right link and cleans that page too, and so on while the pages
it reaches carry its cycle ID.  It clears the cycle ID from each such page it
cleans, so the page is not cleaned a second time.  The clear is WAL-logged,
in the page's XLOG_BARK_VACUUM record (see "WAL" below), where nbtree treats
it as a hint.

A bulk delete takes a cleanup lock on every live leaf, whether or not it
has entries to delete, as nbtree does (see "Deleting index tuples during
VACUUM" in the nbtree README); the scan barkvacuumcleanup runs when there
was no bulk delete deletes no entries and keeps a share lock.  An index-only scan, and any scan with a non-MVCC
snapshot, keeps a pin on the leaf it is returning entries from, so VACUUM
cannot delete a TID the scan has read but not yet returned, and the heap
line pointer the TID refers to cannot be reused while the scan still needs
it.  A plain index scan with an MVCC snapshot drops the pin once it has read
the page (see "Scans" below), as nbtree's does: its snapshot rejects a
recycled line pointer's new tuple, so an idle cursor does not hold up
VACUUM.  Replay of VACUUM's record takes a cleanup lock too,
as nbtree's does, so the interlock also holds for scans on a hot standby:
replay waits for a standby scan's pin, or cancels the scan once
max_standby_streaming_delay has passed.

Bottom-up deletion
------------------

As in nbtree (see "Bottom-Up deletion" in the nbtree README), an insert
that the executor marks indexUnchanged -- a new version from a non-HOT
UPDATE that did not change this index's key -- and that would split a leaf
first runs bark_bottomup_delete (barkdelete.c) on the leaf.  Every heap TID
on the page goes to table_index_delete_tuples with bottomup set; the TIDs of
entries whose key equals the new entry's, and every LIST and POSTING member,
are marked promising.  The table AM visits a few heap blocks and reports the
TIDs whose HOT chains are dead to every snapshot.  Their entries are deleted,
and a LIST or POSTING that keeps some members is re-formed with them, by the
same code VACUUM uses (bark_reform_entry), in one XLOG_BARK_DELETE record.
If that left room for the new entry it is inserted; otherwise the leaf splits
as before.  Coalescing into an equal-key LIST or POSTING still comes first.
In an index with an extracted column one row may have several entries on
the leaf, but the table AM takes each TID at most once (heapam's sort of
the TIDs refuses two equal ones), so the TIDs are sorted and each is
offered once, promising if any of its entries is, with the free space of
all of them; its answer applies to every entry of that TID.  In an index
that stores version tags the answer is per entry, (TID, tag): a row's
stale entries are dead while its current ones live, so a TID is offered
once per tag, and only entries of one TID and one tag share an answer.
(Merging by TID alone made the dead stale entry's answer delete the
row's live entries with it, and an index scan then lost the row.)

OVERSIZED entries are not offered to the table AM: deleting one has to free
its overflow chain once the leaf no longer references it, which VACUUM does.

The deletion runs under the exclusive lock the insert holds, not a cleanup
lock, and its redo takes an exclusive lock too, as nbtree's simple and
bottom-up deletion do (see "Simple deletion" there).  The heap tuples are
dead to every snapshot, and the heap does not reuse their line pointers
until VACUUM, which takes a cleanup lock on every leaf first; so a scan that
copied one of the deleted TIDs before the deletion only finds a dead tuple
at it.  On a hot standby, redo first cancels the queries whose snapshots
could still see those tuples (the record's snapshotConflictHorizon).  No
entry moves to another page, so VACUUM's cycle IDs are unaffected; a leaf
the pass empties is an empty leaf, which VACUUM deletes.

Dead-entry hints and simple deletion
------------------------------------

As in nbtree (see "Simple deletion" in the nbtree README), an index scan
whose heap fetch finds that an entry's row is dead to every snapshot
(heapam sets kill_prior_tuple) remembers the item, and when the scan leaves
the page, by stepping, rescan, restore of a mark on another page or end of
scan, bark_killitems marks LP_DEAD the line pointers of the entries whose
every heap TID was killed: nbtree's _bt_killitems.  The marks are hints.
They are set under the page's share lock through BufferBeginSetHintBits
(which may refuse, while the page is being written out), are not
WAL-logged, and are lost freely: a split does not carry them to either
half, and every in-place rewrite of an entry clears its mark
(BarkPageOverwriteEntry), since the new entry may hold a live TID.  A page
with a newly marked entry gets BARK_HAS_GARBAGE, a hint too, which every
deletion clears; nothing reads it, as nbtree reads its BTP_HAS_GARBAGE
only for indexes older than heap TID order.

Which entries.  A SINGLE when its TID is killed.  A LIST (plain or coded)
or POSTING only when every one of its TIDs was killed by this scan, as
nbtree requires every TID of a posting list tuple: one live member keeps
the entry.  The kills of one entry's members are consecutive items, since
items[] is in index order.  An OVERSIZED entry, whose one TID is in its
ref, when that is killed: scans then skip it without reading its overflow
chain, and VACUUM, not simple deletion, removes it, since only VACUUM frees
chains (as for bottom-up deletion).  A marker names no row and is never
killed; pivots are never returned.  In an index with an extracted column
a row has an entry per key, and only the entry the scan returned the row
from is marked; the others are marked by scans that return the row from
them, or stay for VACUUM.

Finding the entry again.  An item records the offset its entry had when
read.  The scan looks there and to the right (inserts move entries right)
for the entry whose TID range holds the item's TID, and gives up on the
kill if it does not find one (a split moved it, or a deletion before it
shifted it left).  The pin rule is nbtree's (dropPin, "Making concurrent TID
recycling safe" in the nbtree README): a scan that kept its pin since the
read (index-only and non-MVCC scans) blocked VACUUM's cleanup lock, so no
TID on the page can have been removed and given to a new row; a plain MVCC
scan drops its pin after the read, so it records the page's LSN then
(BarkScanPosData.lsn) and marks nothing unless the LSN is unchanged: any
logged change at all, VACUUM's included, gives the kills up.  A held pin
does not keep the page from being emptied by deletion and then deleted
(neither takes a cleanup lock), so a deleted page gives the kills up too.
A scan of a GIN-interface column has no leaf position
(bark_mk_gin_gettuple collects all its TIDs first, with no pin), a bitmap
scan gets no kill_prior_tuple, and a KNN scan's cursors are not currPos:
none of them marks entries, and all of them skip marked ones.

Readers.  bark_readpage and the KNN read skip a marked entry's rows when
scan->ignore_killed_tuples, which is false in a transaction that started in
recovery, as for nbtree: the primary marks by its horizon, which a
standby's snapshots are not part of, and a standby gets the marks in page
images.  bark_readpage does not compare a marked entry's keys, except the
last on the page in the scan's direction, whose keys may still end the scan
there.  The unique check skips a marked entry without a heap fetch, and
marks the entry of a row its fetch finds dead to everyone, as nbtree's
_bt_check_unique does.  amcheck accepts marks anywhere, and heapallindexed
does not fingerprint a marked entry, as verify_nbtree.c does not (except
in a transaction that started in recovery, for the reason above).  The
merge pass runs after simple deletion has deleted the marked entries; one
merged all the same (no heap relation, or past PG_INT16_MAX TIDs) loses its
mark.  An insert does not coalesce into a marked entry; a TID above its
range gets a SINGLE, and a TID inside it first has simple deletion delete
the marked entries, as nbtree's _bt_findinsertloc deletes a dead posting
list that a new TID falls into.

Simple deletion.  An insert that would split a leaf first runs
bark_simple_delete, nbtree's _bt_simpledel_pass, before bottom-up deletion
and the merge, as _bt_delete_or_dedup_one_page orders them; it shares the
deletion pass, and its XLOG_BARK_DELETE record, with bottom-up deletion
(bark_delete_pass, as nbtree's two share _bt_delitems_delete_check).  It
offers the table AM every TID of a marked entry as known deletable, and
every other TID on the page that is in a heap block of theirs or of the new
entry's TID (nbtree's _bt_deadblocks), and deletes or re-forms each entry
by the table AM's answer.  heapam computes the snapshotConflictHorizon from
the known-deletable TIDs' heap tuples too, so redo on a hot standby cancels
the queries whose snapshots could still see the deleted rows, and none
when the rows died before every standby snapshot; a row pruned already has
its horizon in the prune record.  A leaf with no marked entry costs one
pass over its line pointers.

WAL.  bark_mask masks a leaf's line pointer flags and BARK_HAS_GARBAGE, as
btree_mask does, for wal_consistency_checking.  With data checksums or
wal_log_hints, the first hint on a page after a checkpoint writes a
full-page image (MarkBufferDirtyHint), so the standby gets the marks of
that moment; later hints reach it only in a later image.  Nothing else in
BARK depended on pages having no unlogged changes: no code compares a page
with its LSN but bark_killitems, the level-1 cache validates a page by its
flags, level and high key under its lock, never by LSN, the split copies
the original page's LSN into the left half for the full-page-image test
only, and amcheck checks no line pointer flags.  The vacuum cycle ID's
clear stays logged ("WAL").

Versioned entries.  The marking is per entry and is decided by matching an
entry's members to the TIDs the scan killed.  An entry that also carries a
version tag ((key, TID, tag), phase 5b) is reported dead by the table AM
for that tag, and the same matching applies with the tag compared too: a
kill names one (TID, tag) member, an entry is marked when all its members
are killed, and simple deletion offers them as known deletable.  Nothing
here assumes one entry per TID except the one-TID shapes.

Tests: bark_lpdead (bark module): marks after a scan of deleted rows
(pageinspect), backward, index-only and KNN scans that skip them, simple
deletion when the leaf would split, the unique check's marks, LIST, coded
LIST and POSTING entries with partial and full deaths, OVERSIZED entries,
a multikey index, a BARK-implemented btree index, a scan that dropped its
pin and gives its kills up on a changed page, and regress/mvcc's case,
whose index stays 16 kB; bark_lpdead_snapshot (isolation): a snapshot
that sees the rows keeps them; t/014_lpdead_standby.pl: unlogged hints and
the page image after a checkpoint, masking under wal_consistency_checking,
and simple deletion's recovery conflict, or none.

Merging a page's entries before a split
---------------------------------------

As nbtree runs its deduplication pass (_bt_dedup_pass) before splitting a
leaf, an insert into an index whose entries coalesce (see
bark_allequalimage) that would split a leaf first runs bark_merge_page
(barkdelete.c) on it: runs of adjacent entries of one key are merged, in
page order, into fewer, larger LIST or POSTING entries, each up to
BarkMaxItemSize, in the shape bark_form_entry chooses by size.  Entries of
a key are in heap TID order with disjoint ranges, so a merged entry's
members are already ascending.  SINGLE entries fold in, OVERSIZED ones are
skipped, and a merge never spans the new entry's insert offset.  The pass
stops once the page has room for the new entry; if it made room, the entry
is inserted, else the leaf splits as before (the merge stays).  The two
places it runs are the ones where an insert would otherwise split: an entry
that does not fit (after bottom-up deletion, if that ran), and a LIST or
POSTING that a new heap TID falls inside but that can neither take it nor be
divided in place (bark_coalesce_list's BARK_COALESCE_SPLIT), which is what a
reinsert into a CREATE INDEX-built index meets, its LISTs covering the whole
TID range of each page.  After the merge the insert offset is found again,
and the coalesce is tried once more.  The single-value split's cut
(bark_singleval_cut) is left alone: it means to split.

Each group's first entry is overwritten with the merged entry and the rest
are deleted, in one XLOG_BARK_MERGE record.  No heap TID leaves the page and
no entry moves to another page, so the pass needs only the exclusive lock
the insert holds, as nbtree's does: scans copy the items they return under
the page's share lock and reposition by key and heap TID, never by offset,
and VACUUM's cycle IDs are unaffected.  Redo takes an exclusive lock too and
has no recovery conflict to resolve.

Scans
-----

A scan reads a leaf page at a time, as nbtree's _bt_readpage does
(bark_readpage): under the page's share lock it copies every matching heap
TID into the scan position (BarkScanPosData), and for an index-only scan
each matching entry's key into a tuple workspace, then saves the sibling
links and lets the lock go.  The executor is handed items from that copy, so
concurrent inserts and splits on the page cannot make the scan return an
entry twice or skip one.  Moving to the next page follows the saved right
link; moving left goes through bark_lock_and_validate_left, which recovers
from a split or deletion of the left sibling as nbtree's
_bt_lock_and_validate_left does.  A plain MVCC scan drops its pin after the
read (nbtree's dropPin rule); index-only scans and non-MVCC scans keep it.
A parallel scan hands pages out through a shared cursor with nbtree's
seize/release protocol.  Mark and restore copy the position, as nbtree's do;
the executor does not restore a scan with ORDER BY operators, since its
reorder queue would not be rewound.

The copy holds one item per matching entry, not one per heap TID.  A LIST
or POSTING entry's item points at a copy of the entry's body (its TID
array, coded deltas or sbm) in the tuple workspace, and bark_gettuple
returns its members one call at a time, decoding the body into
so->entryTids when it enters the item (a plain array is read in place).
So a LIMIT 1 decodes the one entry it returns from, not the page: since
coded LISTs a duplicate-heavy leaf holds about 3200 TIDs in two dozen
entries, and expanding them all into items made LIMIT 1 cost twice
nbtree's instructions.  Nothing is read from the page after the lock is
released, so resuming within an entry needs no new concurrency rule: a
split, an insert or a VACUUM between two calls changes the page, not the
copy, exactly as between two pages.  The position records the member
returned last (memberIndex), and a mark records it too.  A kill names an
(item, member) run; bark_killitems marks an entry LP_DEAD only when one
run covers every member of the copy and the entry on the page still holds
exactly those TIDs.  A multikey scan filters an entry's members when it
reads the page (bark_mk_accept, bark_mk_seen) and copies the survivors as a
TID array instead.  Bitmap scans and the GIN interface's streams take each
item's members as a batch.

A bitmap scan (bark_getbitmap, and every GIN-interface path that fills a
TIDBitmap) adds TIDs through a BarkTbmBatch: tbm_add_tuples caches a heap
page's entry only within one call, so a call per TID pays a hash lookup
per TID.  The batch, allocated once per scan at 6 kB (below AllocSet's
chunk limit), keeps two arrays of 512 single TIDs, one exact and one to
recheck (a bitmap keeps the recheck flag per heap page, so splitting by it
changes nothing), flushed when one is full and once when amgetbitmap's
additions end.  bark_tbm_add_pos copies a page's items with the counts in
locals, since a store through an ItemPointer may alias them under
-fno-strict-aliasing.  A bitmap is a set and is not read before amgetbitmap
returns, so a leaf boundary needs no flush.  A LIST or POSTING entry's
members are already an array, decoded per item, and go to tbm_add_tuples
directly.  An index scan's own bitmap of an index whose TID stride exceeds
TBM_MAX_TUPLES_PER_PAGE folds each TID before it joins the batch
(bark_mk_gin_tbm_add).  This is the batching of nbtree's btgetbitmap
proposed for core in the "Batch B-tree TIDs when building a bitmap"
thread on pgsql-hackers.

bark_gettuple settles the row returned last at the top of each call (the
kill_prior_tuple test, bark_kill_prior, and the entry verdict of
"Versioned multikey scans") before bark_step_member moves the position:
the position still names that row's (item, member) there, and the table
AM sets the verdict before it asks for the next row (relscan.h).

Versioned multikey scans
------------------------

An index with BARK_META_MKTAGS (see "Version tags") can hold entries of a
row that are valid for different versions: an update that kept the
locator inserted the new version's full key set, tagged, and the old
entries stay until VACUUM removes them.  The page-read seen set, which
keeps a row's first entry by TID, would then keep a stale entry and drop
the valid one (`doc && '{a,b}'` after {a,b} became {b,c} in place loses
the row).  Such a scan, through a table AM, follows rule D instead
(bark_mk_ruled; the decision is the meta page's alone, so tagged entries
already on disk are always handled so):

  * A page read keeps every entry the filters and procedure 9 accept
    (bark_mk_seen only records a group change, as BARK_ITEM_NEWGROUP on
    the item).
  * At the top of bark_gettuple (and bark_knn_gettuple) the entry
    returned last is settled by the table AM's verdict,
    xs_entry_verdict (bark_mk_settle): VALID puts its row in R, the seen
    set's radix tree; INVALID puts (TID, tag) in J, a hash, under an MVCC
    snapshot only; UNKNOWN is an error, since the table AM broke its
    obligation (amlocator.h), and guessing could return a row twice or
    not at all.
  * An entry is then returned only if its row is not in R and its
    (TID, tag) is not in J (bark_mk_admit); it becomes the entry whose
    verdict is due.
  * R and J empty when the scan returns from the first item of a new
    group of the columns before the extracted one, unless the scan is
    marked.  A KNN scan interleaves groups and keeps them whole.
  * bark_markpos settles the due verdict first (the marked row was
    returned); bark_restrpos drops it (the row is behind the mark and is
    returned again) and takes the rows added since the mark out of R.  J
    needs no undo: under an MVCC snapshot its facts hold for the scan.
  * A rescan clears R, J and the due verdict, so a LIMIT, a closed cursor
    or a nested loop's inner rescan, which leave a verdict unread, raise
    nothing.
  * A non-MVCC snapshot keeps R and never fills J.  Under one, heap may
    return several tuples through one entry (xs_heap_continue); the
    verdict is about the entry, and BARK reads it only at its next call.
  * A caller of amgettuple without a table AM (scan->heapRelation NULL)
    gets the page-read seen set: dedup by TID, no verdict.

useseen is decided as before (bark_mk_rescan): when one point boundary,
procedure 8's oneentry or a never-multikey index rules a second matching
entry of one version out, no set is used; a stale entry is rejected by
the table AM whether or not the set is used.  Bitmap scans need none of
this: the bitmap holds each locator once, and the table AM rechecks every
row with history against the original qual.  A GIN-interface index gets
no MKTAGS: its TIDBitmap has no entry to judge.

The scan's memory stays under AllocSet's chunk limit (ALLOC_CHUNK_LIMIT,
8 kB), so it comes from the context's blocks with no malloc of its own:
items[] is at most MaxIndexTuplesPerPage items of 16 bytes, 6.5 kB, and a
plain scan's copied bodies are no larger than the page.  An index-only
scan's workspace also holds the keys, starts at 16 kB as nbtree's does, and
grows past that for a page whose keys decode larger (prefix compression,
OVERSIZED entries); a multikey scan's survivor arrays can grow it too.  The
one allocation that scales with an entry is entryTids: a LIST decodes to at
most 4095 TIDs (24 kB), but a dense POSTING entry can decode to many more.

The first descent uses the longest bound the keys give on the leading
columns (bark_make_bound): equality on columns 1..k-1 and a bound on column
k, compared through the opfamily's cross-type ORDER procs.  A lower bound
sorts before every key equal to it and an upper bound after, so a backward
scan descends straight to its last match.  When column k's key is a strict
inequality (> or <) the roles swap, as nbtree's _bt_first sets nextkey for
>: a forward scan's bound sorts after the keys equal to it and a backward
one's before them, so the scan starts past the entries the key excludes
instead of reading and rejecting them.  A row comparison bounds the
descent with its usable prefix, the members on consecutive columns from its
first one that are not NULL and keep the first member's direction, as
nbtree's _bt_first does, and one on column 1 also ends the scan once an
entry sorts past that prefix.  The prefix is strict only when it is the
whole row: under (a, c) > (5, 10) on (a, b, c) the entries with a = 5 may
match.

A scan stops at the first entry past its bound in its direction
(bark_past_bound), testing columns in order as nbtree's required keys do in
_bt_check_compare: a key that bounds column k in the scan's direction ends
the scan when an entry is past it, provided every earlier column has an
equality key that the entry equals; at the first column without one, or
where the entry differs from it, the test stops, and the entry is left to
the filter.  So under a = 5 AND b < 10 the scan ends at (5, 10), and under
a > 5 AND b < 10 only column 1 ends it.  A row comparison on column 1 ends
the scan the same way on its usable prefix.  Every key, bounding or not, is
also tested on each entry the scan reads (bark_tuple_matches), except the
keys the per-page test below marks satisfied; for some keys that test is
all they do: IS NULL and IS NOT NULL (bark_key_bounds returns false for any
key with SK_ISNULL), a key with a NULL argument, which matches nothing, a
cross-type key whose opfamily has no ORDER proc for its pair of types, and
arrays outside the required keys.  An inequality array (col < ANY (...)) is reduced at rescan
to a plain key on its extreme element (bark_setup_array_keys).

ScalarArrayOp keys.  The equality keys of columns 1..m, each column having
an equality array or a scalar equality, are the scan's required keys when
one of them is an array, as in nbtree.  Their elements, sorted into the
column's key order, form an odometer whose combinations are in index order;
the bound of each descent is the current combination plus column m+1's
range bound.  When an entry that fails the keys sorts after the current
combination, or equals it and is past column m+1's bound, the odometer
moves to the first combination at or after the entry; when that one starts
beyond the page (after the high key forward, before the first entry
backward) the scan re-descends to it, and when none is left the scan ends.
Arrays on later columns only filter.  A parallel scan stops reading a page
the same way but never re-descends.

Skip scan.  With no key on column 1 and a key that bounds column 2, the
scan skips the parts of each column-1 group that cannot match.  At the
first entry that fails the keys, bark_skip_on_page builds the bound of the
next possible match and compares it with the page's high key: past it, the
scan stops reading the page and re-descends to the bound; otherwise a binary
search finds the next possible match on the page.  A page read that ends
without reaching that test decides from its last entry (bark_skip_plan).
Either way the scan re-descends only when the bound sorts after the high
key, so the descent never lands on the page just read.  When column 1's
opclass has skip support (a discrete type such as int4 or date), the scan
goes from a group past column 2's bounds straight to (next value, column
2's lower bound); otherwise it descends past the whole group first.  It
skips forward only, and only over column 1; nbtree also skips over several
leading columns.

On every leaf after a descent's first, bark_readpage first tests the scan
keys against the page's first and last data entries, as nbtree's
_bt_set_startikey does (bark_page_satisfied_keys).  A key on column k that
both satisfy, where the two are equal on columns 1..k-1, holds for every
entry between them, so it is not tested again on that page.  An equality
array is marked when its column holds one value across the page, and that
value is an element; a row comparison when both entries satisfy it on the
same deciding member, as in nbtree.  The first leaf a descent reaches skips
the check, as in nbtree: a selective lookup reads only that page and would
pay for the check without gaining from it.

Ordered-operator (KNN) scans, ORDER BY col <~> const, walk outward from
the constant in two cursors, one in each direction, each reading a page at
a time like the plain scan, and merge them by distance (barkknn.c), with
one ordering operator.  An ordered index keeps a later column's values
spread over the whole index, one group per value of the columns before it,
so the operator is planned on the leading column, or on the column after a
prefix that the path's clauses fix to one value each, inside whose run the
column is in order.  The planner asks the AM which column that is
(amorderbyopcolumn, called from build_index_paths with the path's clauses
and passed to match_pathkeys_to_index).  barkorderbyopcolumn counts the
leading columns fixed by an equality on a btree column, or, on the
extracted column, by its only clause when that has a Const whose procedure
8 boundaries are one point with the same argument at both ends
(bark_cost_boundaries reports it), and answers the column after them,
unless that column is itself extracted.  This is RUM's ORDER BY ts <=> x
after a term qual.  A row's keys are distinct, so a row has at most one
entry in a point's run: the scan needs no seen set and returns each row
once.  An array, a range, several boundaries, a Param or a second clause on
the extracted column fixes nothing.  At rescan bark_knn_fix_column takes
each fixed column's value from the scan keys (bark_mk_point for the
extracted column's point, else a plain equality's argument and ORDER proc)
into a BarkScanBound with the center after it; the descent is
bark_search_bound and the split offset bark_binsrch_bound, as a plain
scan's, and bark_knn_readpage ends a cursor at the first entry outside the
run (bark_knn_in_run).  A scan whose keys do not fix the prefix (a caller
of the AM that is not the planner) raises an error rather than return rows
out of order.  A NULL fixed value, which a generic plan's parameter can
give (tenant = $1), matches nothing, so the run is empty (knn->emptyrun)
and the scan returns no row, as the qual does.
A scroll cursor over a KNN scan is materialized: the scan cannot run its
distance order backward.  A NULL key's distance is infinity, so its rows
come last, and a NULL constant makes every entry's distance infinity: the
scan then returns every row, as a Sort over the NULL distances would; in
a run, every row of the run (the center's procs entry is NULL, so the
descent lands on the run's NULL keys).

An ordering operator of a multikey class on a leading extracted column is
not a KNN scan (bark_mk_order_setup in barkscan.c).  Procedure 8 is called
with the ordering key and returns the boundaries to walk, normally all of
the column, with BarkQueryFlags.searchnulls for the NULL entries and
BarkQueryFlags.backward for a descending order.  The boundaries become the
scan's one required key, as a search key's do, and the scan walks them
with the ordinary page reads in the direction that gives that key order
(backward XOR the column's DESC), with the seen set.  So a row comes back
once, at its first entry, and that entry's key is its exact ORDER BY value
(xs_orderbyvals; the operator must return the key type).  The NULL result
sorts last; when the column's NULLS option puts the NULL entries first in
the walk, the NULL boundary is held back and read by a second positioning
once the keys are done (bark_mk_order_rest), the seen set dropping the
rows already returned.  When procedure 8 also sets BarkQueryFlags.orderrest,
its boundaries do not hold every row (a wildcard class's path range misses
the documents without that path): the second positioning then reads the
rest of the column, every key and the NULL entries, and the rows not yet
returned come back with a NULL ORDER BY value, last.  The seen set is then
used even for an index that is not multikey, since a row met in the
boundaries is met again in the rest, and the boundaries' entries are read
twice.  Search keys on the column do not position or
filter this walk (a row met first at a failing key would come back later,
out of order); every row comes back with xs_recheck and the executor
applies them.

Cost estimate
-------------

barkcostestimate (bark.c) is btcostestimate applied to the quals BARK's
scan uses to position and stop it, the "boundary quals"; the others only
filter.  As in nbtree, a column's quals bound the scan only when every
earlier column has an equality.  The walk differs from btcostestimate's
where BARK's scan does less:

  * IS NULL and IS NOT NULL never bound (NullTest clauses are skipped).
  * An equality ScalarArrayOp bounds like an equality, on any column of
    the equality prefix and in either direction, and the scan descends once
    per combination of the arrays' elements; a column with several
    equalities counts the one with the fewest elements.  The number of
    descents is clamped to a third of the index's pages, as btcostestimate
    clamps it.
  * A row comparison ends the walk; past column 1 it counts only when it
    bounds the end where the scan starts, and not in a skip scan.
  * A skip scan (bark_cost_skips: forward, no qual on column 1, a bounding
    qual on column 2) costs one descent per distinct column-1 value plus
    one, unless the number of values is a default guess or exceeds the
    index's pages; only column 2's plain quals bound it.
  * A KNN scan (ORDER BY operators) treats every qual as a filter, except
    the quals on the columns before the ordered one, which fix them
    (barkorderbyopcolumn) and bound it as an equality prefix does; the
    extracted column's point counts as an equality.

The rows read are the boundary quals' selectivity times the heap's rows, so
LIST and POSTING entries lower the cost through the index's smaller page
count, which genericcostestimate prorates over, as nbtree's posting lists
do.  A unique index with equality on every column reads one row.  The
descent charge (ceil(log2(tuples)) comparisons, plus 50 cpu_operator_cost
per level, the leaf included, per descent), the meta page as the only
non-leaf page counted, and the correlation (btcost_correlation, exported
from selfuncs.c with examine_indexcol_variable and
DEFAULT_PAGE_CPU_MULTIPLIER, so the two AMs share it) are btcostestimate's.
On top, each entry read is charged a random page read per overflow page,
estimated from the index's average bytes per row beyond BarkMaxItemSize:
the expected charge for an index of uniformly large entries, nothing for
one with none, and the average for a mixed one, since the index keeps no
count of its OVERSIZED entries.

An index with an extracted column has K entries per row: the meta page's
bark_nkeys over the heap's rows, as of the last CREATE INDEX or VACUUM, or
1 while BARK_META_MULTIKEY is clear (bark_cost_keys_per_row reads the meta
page as gincostestimate reads GIN's; a hypothetical index has K = 1 and is
taken to need the seen set).  The extracted column's first qual positions
the scan and ends the walk; its later quals filter.  bark_cost_boundaries
runs procedure 8 on the qual's constant, as gincost_pattern runs GIN's
extractQuery, and counts one descent per boundary (one for a qual whose
argument is not a constant); an equality ScalarArrayOp there counts one per
element.  The entries read are the rows times K, capped at the index's
entries, each charged cpu_index_tuple_cost, plus cpu_operator_cost per
entry for the seen set when the scan needs it (the flag is set, the
boundaries are not a single point and procedure 8 did not set oneentry) and
again when procedure 8 asked for
procedure 9.  The rows returned stay the quals' selectivity times the
heap's rows, since the scan returns each row once, and the correlation is
0 when the extracted column is first: its statistics are of values, not
keys.

A path that needs the seen set and whose rows per group of the fixed
columns, at 16 bytes each, exceed get_hash_memory_limit() gets
disabled_nodes incremented, so the planner takes a bitmap scan (which never
builds the set) or a sequential scan.  The rows per group are the scan's
rows, divided by the extracted column's descents when every fixed column
has an equality.  disable_cost would be wrong here: cost_bitmap_tree_node
reuses the IndexPath's indextotalcost for the bitmap path, so the penalty
would rule out the bitmap scan too, while the bitmap path does not inherit
the IndexPath's disabled_nodes.  An operator class that declares an
operator on its extracted column but has no procedure 8 is costed as one
boundary; a scan using it raises an error.

An ordered scan on the extracted column (ORDER BY its ordering operator)
follows the KNN rule and counts every entry.  The column's quals neither
position nor filter it, and every row it returns is rechecked, so its heap
fetches are the rows the other columns' quals pass, not the column quals'
selectivity: with a LIMIT it beats a sort, and a filter on the column alone
does not make it look cheap.

VACUUM's num_index_tuples is the count of live heap TIDs, as nbtree's is,
so reltuples (which plancat uses for a partial index's tuples) has the
heap's unit.

Tools
-----

contrib/amcheck/verify_bark.c: bark_index_check(index, heapallindexed)
walks every page under AccessShareLock and checks the meta page's version,
key order within each page, entries within the high key (strictly when
attributes were truncated, and below its heap TID for an equal leaf entry),
heap TID order among equal keys, sibling links both ways with matching
levels, each right sibling's first key against the page's high key, each
downlink's child level and first key, no unfinished split, entry sizes (no
leaf entry over BarkMaxItemSize, no SINGLE large enough to be OVERSIZED, a
POSTING entry no smaller than its removal bound), the deleted-page layout,
BARK_PREFIX pages and their coded entries, OVERSIZED entries' chains read
in full, and markers (the reserved TID only on a SINGLE of an index whose
procedure 7 can return markers).  Each cross-page check holds share locks
on both pages in an order the writers use.  heapallindexed fingerprints
each member of each leaf entry, as the SINGLE it would be alone, in a Bloom
filter and probes it with each tuple a new CREATE INDEX would index, as
bt_index_check does; it is refused for an index with an extracted column,
whose rows have several entries.  bark_index_parent_check takes ShareLock
and also walks the tree level by level from the root: each level's
downlinks must name, in order, exactly the pages of the level below, each
equal to the high key of the child before it, and each parent's high key
equal to its last child's.  It then follows every OVERSIZED entry's chain,
leaf entries', high keys' and downlinks', to its end by the bark_next links
(bark_check_chains): a chain may not reach a page that is not an overflow
page, reach a page a chain already reached (a loop, or two chains that
cross), or be longer than its entry needs.  A chain is followed once
however many pivots share it.  An overflow page that no chain reaches is
reported with a WARNING, by orphaned chain (its first page and its number
of pages), and not as corruption: a chain is written before the entry
that references it, so a crash, or an error such as running out of disk
space, between the two leaves the chain's pages unreferenced (see
XLOG_BARK_OVERFLOW), as does a crash while VACUUM frees a chain page by
page.  Every other path frees a chain it stops referencing: VACUUM the
chain of each OVERSIZED entry it removes, and of the downlink the deletion
of an empty leaf removes, and an insert that writes a chain and then gives
up the leaf it found by the fast path (bark_insert_key) the chain it
wrote.  A deleted overflow page, which VACUUM freed and
which awaits recycling, is not an overflow page and is not reported.
Nothing reclaims orphaned pages but REINDEX (follow-up R4c: VACUUM would
free a chain no entry references once the transaction that wrote it is
over).  pg_amcheck checks BARK
indexes, and btree indexes BARK implements, when the database's amcheck
is version 1.7 or later (prepare_btree_command).

With checkunique (amcheck 1.8), both functions also walk the leaves of a
unique index by right links and report a run of equal key columns, which
may cross leaves, holding two entries whose heap rows are both visible to
the check's snapshot (bark_check_unique_runs, as bt_entry_unique_check).
For an index with an extracted column the run is of the fixed columns and
one key; markers and keys with a NULL column (unless NULLS NOT DISTINCT)
are skipped, and OVERSIZED keys are compared in full.  pg_amcheck
--checkunique passes it through for BARK indexes from amcheck 1.8.

amcheck's checks of one page by itself are bark_page_verify
(barkverify.c), which the backend shares; the checks of a page against
another page (sibling links, a downlink against its child) and the walk
of the tree stay in verify_bark.c.

Page validator.  In cassert builds every change to a BARK page is
followed, once its critical section is over and while the page is still
exclusive-locked, by bark_verify_page_change on each page the change
touched: bark_page_verify's SHAPES checks (layout, entry shapes and
sizes, LIST and POSTING members; no catalog access, no comparator) and
ORDER checks (key order, heap TID order among equal keys, the high-key
bound), and for the meta page its magic number and version and for an
overflow page its layout.  The idea is RethinkDB's: its debug builds run
a node's full check after every mutation (leaf::validate in
src/btree/leaf_node.cc), so a fault is reported by the call that made
it.  The call sites are every function that ends a critical section on
a BARK page: bark_insert_entry, bark_insert_multi, bark_overwrite_entry,
bark_add_tid_entry, bark_swap_tid_entry, bark_split (both halves, the
right sibling, the child), bark_new_root (with the old root and the meta
page), bark_create_root_leaf, bark_delitems_vacuum, bark_delitems_delete
(bottom-up deletion and the merge), bark_delete_empty_leaf (four pages),
bark_write_overflow_chain, bark_free_oversized, bark_set_multikey and
bark_set_nkeys.

A change of one entry or a few adjacent ones (the first five of those
functions) checks the shapes of only those entries, their order against
the entry on each side and the high key, and the page's layout and high
key; every 32nd such check in a backend checks the whole page
(BARK_VERIFY_FULL_EVERY), and every other change is checked whole.  So
a fault in or next to the changed entry is reported by the statement
that made it, and one elsewhere on the page by a later change's
whole-page check or by amcheck.  A failure is an ERROR with the caller
in its context, not a PANIC: the change is logged by then, so a PANIC
would only restart the server into replaying it; the ERROR fails the
statement that broke the page, and amcheck reports the page again.
CONTENTS (overflow chains, markers) and COMPLETE (no unfinished split)
are not run there.

WAL redo runs the SHAPES checks (bark_verify_redo), with no relcache
entry and so no comparator, and PANICs on a failure: on every page of
every record, but on a record that changed only a few entries just one
time in 32, whole.  Redo of bark/t/008_redo_dropped_index.pl, 41,000
ADD_TID records into large LIST and POSTING entries, takes 5.2 s under
cassert and 28 s with gcov without the validator; checking every such
record whole took 9.4 s and 55 s, and checking only its touched entry
9.3 s and 55.9 s (an sbm's validation costs as much as redo's own
add-TID), close to pg_ctl's 60 s start wait; one in 32 takes 5.7 s and
31 s.  The primary checked each of those changes when it made it.

The developer option bark_verify_pages (a boolean, PGC_SUSET, cassert
builds only, on by default there) turns both off; elsewhere the calls
compile away.  t/015_page_validator.pl in src/test/modules/bark plants
faults through the injection points bark-verify-fault-count,
bark-verify-fault-order, bark-verify-fault-far, bark-verify-fault-meta
and bark-verify-fault-overflow (t/008_bark_mktags.pl of
test_versioned_tableam through bark-verify-fault-mktags, -converting and
-settled), and bark-verify-force-full makes the next check of a few
entries a whole-page one.

Its cost, in a cassert build on c7i.4xlarge: a write-only workload of
long equal-key LISTs (60,000 inserts into a table with three BARK
indexes, then non-HOT updates, deletes, VACUUM and more inserts) took
5.6x as long with the validator as without (39.5 s against 7.1 s) when
every change checked the whole page, 1.9x (13.6 s) with the bounded
check, and takes 1.5x (10.7 s) now that the shapes of only the touched
entries are checked; the bark, bark_multikey, bark_wildcard and amcheck
test suites took 1.17x (186 s against 159 s, median of 4, measured with
every page checked whole).

contrib/pageinspect/barkfuncs.c: bark_metap, bark_page_stats,
bark_multi_page_stats and bark_page_items (by relation and block, or of a
raw page), which reports each item's shape, its heap TIDs (a LIST's or
POSTING's in full), a pivot's downlink and heap TID, an OVERSIZED entry's
first overflow block, and a prefix-compressed leaf's PREFIX item, with the
data decoded.  contrib/pgstattuple/pgstatindex.c: pgstatbarkindex reports
pgstatindex's columns, computed the same way, plus overflow pages and the
leaf entries of each shape.

Page deletion and recycling
---------------------------

VACUUM reads the index once, in physical order, as nbtree's btvacuumscan
does (bark_vacuum_scan): the same pass that deletes dead entries counts the
entries left and the deleted pages, records in the FSM the deleted pages that
are safe to reuse, and notes each leaf it leaves empty.  barkvacuumcleanup
does nothing after a bulk delete; when no bulk delete ran (no dead tuples
this cycle) it runs the same pass without deleting entries, and marks its
count estimated, so VACUUM leaves reltuples alone.  So VACUUM's cleanup is
cheap after a bulk delete, as nbtree's is, and BARK declares
VACUUM_OPTION_PARALLEL_COND_CLEANUP as nbtree does.  Either way
barkvacuumcleanup caps the count at the heap's when that is exact and the
index has no extracted column (as btvacuumcleanup does: concurrent splits
can make the walk count an entry twice), and stores it in the meta page's
bark_nkeys.  The count is of live heap TIDs, not entries, as
btvacuumpage's is, so reltuples has the heap's unit.

At the end of the pass, VACUUM deletes the empty interior leaves it noted
(bark_delete_empty_leaf): in one XLOG_BARK_UNLINK_PAGE record it
splices the leaf out of the sibling chain, points the leaf's downlink in the
parent at the right sibling and removes the right sibling's old downlink (so
the leaf's key space moves right, as in nbtree), and turns the leaf into a
deleted page.  Only a leaf with siblings on both sides that is not its
parent's last child is deleted; leftmost and rightmost empty leaves, a last
child and emptied internal pages stay.  The parent is found by descending
with the leaf's high key and matching its downlink
(bark_find_parent_downlink) and is locked before the three leaves, the
reverse of a split's child-before-parent order, so the leaves are only
locked conditionally and a busy leaf is left for a later VACUUM; nbtree
avoids the inversion by marking the leaf half-dead first
(_bt_mark_page_halfdead), which BARK does not do.
VACUUM also deletes the overflow pages of the OVERSIZED entries it removes
(bark_free_oversized), and of the right sibling's downlink, which the deletion
of an empty leaf removes, when it is OVERSIZED.

A deleted page has no items (pd_lower = SizeOfPageHeaderData) and holds a
BarkDeletedPageData at the end of the tuple area (pd_upper = pd_special -
MAXALIGN(sizeof(BarkDeletedPageData))); BARK_DELETED is its only flag, and
its level and cycle ID are zero.  BarkPageSetDeleted reinitializes the page
in this form from its links and safexid alone, so that VACUUM and WAL redo
build the same page.
BarkDeletedPageData holds safexid, the next full transaction ID read while
the page and its neighbours were locked for the deletion.  nbtree puts its
BTDeletedPageData at the start of the tuple area under pd_lower; BARK puts it
at the end so that a reader which does not test the flag sees an empty page.

The page keeps the sibling links it had (an overflow page keeps its link to
the next chunk).  A descent that read the parent's downlink, or a scan that
read a sibling link, before the deletion can arrive at the page afterwards;
it moves right through it, as nbtree's readers move through P_IGNORE pages.
Its neighbours no longer link back to it, which amcheck allows.  A page's
right link, read under its lock, never names a deleted page: deletion holds
that page's lock while it rewrites the link.  So a writer that moves right
with its page still locked (bark_lock_right) never meets a deleted sibling,
and only leaves are deleted, so neither does a writer at an internal
level; their tests for one are defensive.

A deleted page is reused only when BarkPageIsRecyclable says so, which is
nbtree's BTPageIsRecyclable test: GlobalVisCheckRemovableFullXid(heaprel,
safexid).  Any backend that might hold a link to the page took its snapshot
before the deletion, so its xmin is no later than safexid; once safexid is
removable by that horizon, no such backend remains.  VACUUM keeps the leaves
it deletes in a pending list and records in the FSM, at its end, those whose
safexid is already removable (nbtree's _bt_pendingfsm_finalize).  VACUUM's page
walk (the bulk delete's, or the cleanup-only scan's) records every other
deleted page in the FSM once it becomes recyclable, so a page not
recyclable at one VACUUM is recorded by a later one; it also counts the
deleted pages (pages_deleted).  bark_get_free_page
repeats the test before it reuses an FSM page, since the FSM is not crash-
safe.  Every path that allocates a page passes the index's heap relation for
the test: inserts and VACUUM directly, and the paths reached from a descent
(bark_finish_split, the overflow chains of OVERSIZED pivots) through
BarkKeyInfo.heaprel, which the insert or VACUUM that started the descent set.

A page reused from the FSM is reused on a hot standby too, by replay of the
record that reinitializes it, and a standby query may still hold a link to
it: the standby's snapshots are not part of the primary's horizon unless
hot_standby_feedback is on.  So before reusing a deleted page,
bark_get_free_page writes an XLOG_BARK_REUSE_PAGE record carrying the page's
safexid, as nbtree's _bt_allocbuf does, whenever wal_level allows hot standby.
Its replay cancels every standby query whose snapshot's xmin is not after
safexid (ResolveRecoveryConflictWithSnapshotFullXid), which is the standby's
version of the GlobalVisCheckRemovableFullXid test the primary made.

safexid is ReadNextFullTransactionId() rather than an assigned XID, as in
nbtree, so a deleted page becomes recyclable only after some transaction that
starts after the deletion takes an XID and ends: on an idle system, pages
deleted by one VACUUM are recorded by the next one after the next write.

WAL
---

BARK has its own WAL resource manager, "Bark" (RM_BARK_ID; barkxlog.h,
barkxlog.c, rmgrdesc/barkdesc.c), for inserts, splits, new roots,
overflow chains, VACUUM, bottom-up deletion, merges and page deletion; CREATE INDEX logs the pages it
writes whole.  Each change to a page is in exactly one record.  The
records:

  XLOG_BARK_INSERT_LEAF (bark_insert_entry, from bark_insert_key)
      One leaf: the new entry and its offset, as nbtree's INSERT_LEAF.  An
      OVERSIZED entry's overflow chain is written first, in its own
      XLOG_BARK_OVERFLOW records.  Redo adds the entry with PageAddItem under an exclusive lock.

  XLOG_BARK_INSERT_MULTI (bark_insert_multi, from bark_insert_entries)
      One leaf: several new entries of one multikey row, coded, and their
      offsets, each counting the entries before it, so redo adds them in
      order as the primary did.  bark_plan_multi picks the run: entries
      that are not oversized, need no unique check, belong on the leaf,
      coalesce into nothing and fit.  A run of one is an INSERT_LEAF.
  XLOG_BARK_INSERT_UPPER (bark_insert_entry, from bark_insert_parent)
      Two pages: the parent, with the downlink to the right half of a split
      and its offset, and the left half, whose BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT flag is
      cleared, as nbtree's INSERT_UPPER.  The primary holds both locks;
      redo, like btree_xlog_insert, clears the flag first and then adds the
      downlink, one page lock at a time, since only writers look at the flag.
      A parent that must split to take the downlink is logged as a split.

  XLOG_BARK_SPLIT (bark_split)
      Up to four pages: the left half (the original block), the new right
      half, the original right sibling, whose left link now names the right
      half, and, for an internal page taking a child's downlink, that child,
      whose BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT flag is cleared.  The record holds the
      level, a leaf flag, the cycle ID stamped on both halves and the
      original page's two sibling links.  As in nbtree, the right half is
      logged whole and the left half is not.  The right half's data is its
      tuple area (pd_upper to pd_special), which the primary builds by
      adding the entries in offset order to an empty temporary page, and
      redo re-adds them the same way (bark_restore_page, as nbtree's
      _bt_restore_page), so every entry lands at the offset and position it
      has on the primary.  The left half is rebuilt from the original page,
      which redo has (it is the same block), as btree_xlog_split does: the
      record carries the offset of the first entry that moved right, the new
      high key, the new entry if it went left, and the replacement of a
      divided or cut entry if that stayed left, both coded for the original
      page; primary and redo both build the half with bark_split_build_left,
      which copies the original page's entries as they are stored, PREFIX
      item included.  A leaf split whose left half takes a prefix other than
      the original page's (see "Leaf prefix compression") re-codes every
      entry, so that half is then logged whole, like the right half, and a
      record flag says so.  The left block is registered as a standard
      page, so the first split after a checkpoint carries its full-page
      image instead.  Redo clears the child's flag first, then holds the
      right half, the left half and the right sibling locked together, as
      btree_xlog_split does.

  XLOG_BARK_NEWROOT (bark_new_root)
      Three pages: the new root, rebuilt from its two downlinks as a split
      half is; the old root, the left child, whose split the new root
      finishes; and the meta page, which gets the new root and level.

  XLOG_BARK_CREATE_ROOT (bark_create_root_leaf)
      Two pages: the first root of an empty index, an empty leaf, and the
      meta page.  CREATE INDEX always writes a root, so today only an
      unlogged index reset to its init fork reaches bark_create_root_leaf,
      and an unlogged index writes no WAL; the record covers any logged
      index that is ever left without a root.

  XLOG_BARK_OVERWRITE (bark_overwrite_entry, from bark_coalesce_list)
      One leaf: an entry's offset and its replacement: a SINGLE or LIST
      re-encoded as a LIST or POSTING by bark_coalesce_list's general path.
      Primary and redo both call PageIndexTupleOverwrite, which keeps the
      offset and moves only the entries stored below the replaced one.  The
      record carries just the new entry, where a generic delta covered every
      byte that moved (pd_upper to the entry).

  XLOG_BARK_ADD_TID (bark_add_tid_entry, from bark_coalesce_list)
      One leaf: an entry's offset and one heap TID added to it, by either
      fast path (a LIST append, or an add to a POSTING set).  Like nbtree's
      INSERT_POST, the record carries only the TID: redo calls
      bark_entry_add_tid on the entry, as the primary did, and overwrites
      the entry with the result.  bark_entry_add_tid depends on nothing but
      the entry and the TID (it takes the key from the entry, not from the
      inserted tuple), and the index's TID stride, so both sides build the
      same bytes; wal_consistency_checking checks that they do.  Redo has no
      meta page to read the stride from, so the record of an index whose
      stride is not MaxHeapTuplesPerPage carries it, a uint16 after
      xl_bark_add_tid; a heap index's records are as they were.

  XLOG_BARK_INSERT_SWAP (bark_swap_tid_entry, from bark_coalesce_list)
      One leaf: an entry's offset and a heap TID that falls inside the
      entry's TID range but does not fit in it (see "Heap TID order").  Like
      ADD_TID, it carries only the TID: redo divides the entry with
      bark_entry_swap_tid, as the primary did, and puts the two parts at the
      entry's offset and the next.  The TID stride follows as for ADD_TID.  nbtree logs the same change as an
      INSERT_POST with a posting offset.  When the parts do not fit, the
      insert splits the page instead, and the SPLIT record carries them.

  XLOG_BARK_VACUUM (bark_delitems_vacuum)
      One leaf: the offsets of entries deleted whole, and the offsets and new
      contents of LIST and POSTING entries rewritten with the members that
      survive (nbtree logs which TIDs left a posting list instead and re-forms
      it in redo; re-forming a POSTING entry needs the tuple descriptor, which
      redo lacks).  Redo rewrites, then deletes, then clears the cycle ID.
      Redo takes a cleanup lock, as btree_xlog_vacuum does.  A page with
      nothing to delete gets a record only when it carries the running
      VACUUM's cycle ID, so a VACUUM that finds nothing logs nothing.

  XLOG_BARK_DELETE (bark_delitems_delete, from bark_simple_delete and
                    bark_bottomup_delete)
      One leaf, as nbtree's XLOG_BTREE_DELETE: the same payload as
      XLOG_BARK_VACUUM, plus the conflict horizon of the heap tuples whose
      entries go and whether the heap is a catalog for logical decoding.
      In hot standby, redo first resolves the snapshot conflict, then
      rewrites and deletes under an exclusive lock (see "Bottom-up
      deletion").  The page's vacuum cycle ID is left alone.

  XLOG_BARK_MERGE (bark_delitems_delete, from bark_merge_page)
      One leaf, as nbtree's XLOG_BTREE_DEDUP is a record of its own: the
      payload of XLOG_BARK_VACUUM, the offsets of the entries absorbed and
      the offsets and contents of the merged entries.  The changes are made
      in the other order, deletions first, since a merged entry is larger
      than the entry it replaces and fits on a full page only once the
      absorbed entries' space is free; the merged entries' offsets are
      numbered as after the deletions.  Redo takes an exclusive lock and
      shares bark_redo_delitems with VACUUM and DELETE.

  XLOG_BARK_UNLINK_PAGE (bark_delete_empty_leaf, bark_lower_bound)
      One opcode for two operations, since the rmgr has no free opcode;
      they are told apart by XLH_BARK_UNLINK_LOWER_SEP in the record's flags
      and by the absence of block 0 in the second.  pg_waldump and
      pg_walinspect (bark_identify sees only the info byte) show both as
      UNLINK_PAGE; the description of the second begins "lower separator".

      The deletion of an empty leaf: four pages, the target leaf
      (reinitialized as a deleted page from the record's links and safexid,
      no image needed), its left sibling's right link, its right sibling's
      left link, and the parent, where the target's downlink is pointed at
      the right sibling and the right sibling's own downlink is removed.
      This is nbtree's MARK_PAGE_HALFDEAD and UNLINK_PAGE in one record,
      since BARK deletes only leaves whose right sibling has the same
      parent.  Redo takes exclusive locks on the left sibling, target, right
      sibling and parent, in that order, and holds them all until the record
      is applied.

      The lowering of a leaf's lower bound (XLH_BARK_UNLINK_LOWER_SEP, see
      "Lowering a lower bound" in "Version tags"): block 1, the left page,
      with its new high key; block 2, the right page, whose
      BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED is cleared; block 3, the parent, with the new
      downlink at poffset.  safexid is unused.  A flag-only clear has block
      2 alone.  Redo locks the left page, right page and parent in that
      order, holds them until the record is applied, and checks each page.

  XLOG_BARK_OVERFLOW (bark_write_overflow_chain)
      Up to XLR_NORMAL_MAX_BLOCK_ID new overflow pages, each with its next
      link and its slice of the oversized tuple; redo rebuilds each page
      from those.  A chain longer than that takes several records, written
      before the entry that refers to the chain, so a crash in between
      leaves only unreferenced pages.

  XLOG_BARK_MARK_DELETED (bark_free_oversized)
      One overflow page, reinitialized as a deleted page that keeps its link
      to the next chunk.  Overflow pages are not in the tree, so the page has
      no siblings to fix, and UNLINK_PAGE's four-page layout would not fit.
      Redo takes an exclusive lock on the page.

  XLOG_BARK_REUSE_PAGE (bark_get_free_page)
      No pages.  The relation, block and the page's safexid as
      snapshotConflictHorizon, plus whether the heap is a catalog for
      logical decoding.  In hot standby, redo resolves the recovery conflict
      (see "Page deletion and recycling").  The page's reinitialization is in
      the allocating record that follows.

The meta page's bark_flags and bark_nkeys are logged as a full page image
(log_newpage_buffer, an XLOG_FPI record) when they change: the multikey flag
once in an index's life, bark_nkeys at most once per VACUUM.

The vacuum cycle ID's clear is logged, in XLOG_BARK_VACUUM, and a split
logs the cycle ID it stamps, although nbtree treats the clear as a hint and
masks the field in btree_mask.  The clear could become a hint, with
bark_mask masking the field; logging it keeps the field identical between
primary and standby.  BARK's unlogged changes are the dead-entry hints: a
leaf entry's LP_DEAD mark and BARK_HAS_GARBAGE (see "Dead-entry hints and
simple deletion").  So bark_mask (wal_consistency_checking) masks the LSN,
checksum, page-header hint bits, the unused space, a leaf's line pointer
flags and BARK_HAS_GARBAGE, as btree_mask does, and not the cycle ID.

Entry encoding (the C-KEYSHAPE contract)
----------------------------------------

A BARK entry is a normal IndexTupleData: the key columns follow the tuple
header.  What varies is the value side.  BARK reuses PostgreSQL's per-AM
reserved bit, INDEX_AM_RESERVED_BIT in t_info, as an "alternate t_tid" marker
exactly as nbtree does.  When the bit is clear, t_tid is a plain heap locator.
When it is set, t_tid is repurposed: its offset-number field is split into a
high-nibble status region (BARK_STATUS_OFFSET_MASK) and a low twelve-bit count
(BARK_OFFSET_MASK), the same partition nbtree uses.

There are five entry shapes (BarkEntryShape):

  SINGLE   The plain nbtree shape.  The alt-TID bit is clear and t_tid holds a
           single heap locator.  An index that does not coalesce (a unique
           index, one with INCLUDE columns, or one whose keys are not all
           equalimage) has only this shape and OVERSIZED.  A marker is a
           SINGLE (see "Multikey keys").

  LIST     Sorted duplicates -- the libdb shape.  The alt-TID bit is set with
           BARK_IS_LIST; the locators are stored in ascending order in the
           tuple body after the key, and the low offset bits hold their count.
           Used when a key maps to a modest number of rows.

  POSTING  An inverted locator set.  The alt-TID bit is set with
           BARK_IS_POSTING; the set is stored as an sbm serialization (see
           lib/sbm.h) in the tuple body after the key: a uint16 holding the
           serialization's length, the serialization, and zero padding up to
           the entry's size.  The entry is sized for the set's removal bound
           (sbm_removal_bound), the largest serialization any subset of the
           set can have; see the invariants below.  Used when the set is large
           and clustered enough that sbm's compression wins even with that
           reserve, which costs at most one bit per locator of a dense run, so
           a scattered set stays a LIST.

  PIVOT    A downlink or high-key tuple on an internal page (or a leaf high
           key).  The alt-TID bit is set with neither list nor posting status;
           t_tid carries the child block number, and the low offset bits hold
           the number of key attributes actually present, since a pivot may be
           shortened by suffix truncation.  BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID says the
           pivot also has a heap TID, the last 6 bytes of its MAXALIGNed
           size (BarkPivotGetHeapTID).

  OVERSIZED  A key (or key + INCLUDE payload) whose formed tuple exceeds
           BarkMaxItemSize.  The alt-TID bit is set with BARK_IS_LIST and
           BARK_IS_POSTING together (a code point no other shape uses).  The
           full index tuple is stored out-of-line on a chain of BARK_OVERFLOW
           pages; the on-page entry is a bare header plus a BarkOverflowRef
           (full byte length, heap locator or downlink, and -- for a pivot --
           its key-attribute count and heap TID), with the first overflow
           block in t_tid.
           This is what lets BARK index a key larger than ~1/3 page, where
           nbtree errors: comparison (so descent, scan positioning and the
           uniqueness check) fetches the full value from the chain
           (bark_fetch_oversized), and an index-only scan returns it, so an
           oversized key behaves exactly like an inline key of the same
           value.  VACUUM needs only the heap locator in the ref, and frees
           the chain of an entry it removes (bark_free_oversized).  A normal-size entry never uses
           this shape, so it is pure overhead-free opt-in.  When the first
           key column is text under a C/POSIX collation, the ref also keeps
           that column's first 32 bytes, decompressed when the full tuple
           stores the value compressed (bark_value_prefix).  They order the
           entry without reading its chain whenever they differ from the
           other side's leading bytes: another OVERSIZED entry, an inline
           key, an insert's key (detoasted once per key), or a scan bound
           (its argument detoasted once per rescan).  Descent, leaf
           positioning, the insert's search and the end of a scan
           (bark_prefix_past_bound) all use them, so a lookup reads the
           chain of the entry it returns and no other.  A tie reads the
           chain, unless both sides are whole and column 1 is the only
           column compared.  No other column
           gets them, even under "C": bpchar ignores trailing spaces, arrays
           compare element by element, and name is a cstring, so their bytes
           are not their order (bark_first_column_bytewise).

The encoding of a leaf entry is chosen per entry, by size (bark_form_entry):
one heap TID is a SINGLE; more are a POSTING when its size, reserve
included, is smaller than the LIST's, plain or coded (bark_form_posting
decides from the removal bound, sbm_removal_bound_sorted, and the coded
LIST's length, before building the sbm), else a LIST, which holds at most
BARK_LIST_MAX_COUNT TIDs; neither is made larger than BarkMaxItemSize.

A LIST is plain or coded (V6; bark_form_list_best keeps the smaller).  A
plain LIST is an ItemPointerData array.  A coded LIST sets BARK_LIST_CODED
(the 0x1000 status bit, BARK_PIVOT_META on a pivot, never set on a leaf
entry) and holds a BarkCodedListHdr (the varbyte stream's length and the
last TID raw), the first TID raw, then the varbyte deltas of the rest, each
TID mapped to block << 11 | offset as ginpostinglist.c does (more offset
bits for a TID stride past 2047; see "TID stride").  The first and
last TIDs stay readable in O(1) for bark_entry_tid_range.  Every reader goes
through bark_entry_get_tids / bark_entry_count_tids / bark_entry_has_tid, and
bark_entry_add_tid splices a TID into a coded LIST's delta stream without
decoding the set (one delta appended past the last TID, the common case, or
the spanning delta replaced by two), giving the same bytes as decoding,
inserting and re-encoding: the coded form is a function of the sorted TID set
alone, so redo is deterministic, and assert builds check the splice against
the full re-form.  Plain
LISTs are unchanged, so no REINDEX is needed.  A subset of a coded LIST never
codes longer than the whole (a removed member's two deltas become their sum),
but a plain subset can be longer than the coded original, so VACUUM re-forms
a coded LIST as a coded LIST (bark_reform_list) to keep the in-place rewrite
within the old slot.  An insert that adds a TID to a LIST or POSTING
entry keeps its shape when it can (bark_entry_add_tid), and a POSTING
entry never turns back into a LIST on insert.
This is what lets a scalar index and an inverted (GIN/RUM-style) index share
one access method and one page format, rather than needing an AM apiece.

Insert coalesces a row into an equal key's entry only in a non-unique index
whose equal keys always have equal images (bark_allequalimage, nbtree's
_bt_allequalimage rule: no INCLUDE columns, and every key opclass's
equalimage function says yes).  CREATE INDEX applies the same rule.  The
sort hands bark_load each run of equal keys with its heap TIDs ascending, as
nbtsort.c's _bt_load sees them when it deduplicates, and the run is written
as the smaller of LIST and POSTING for the longest prefix whose entry fits in
a tenth of a page, the limit _bt_load puts on a posting list, then the same
for the rest of the run.  Entries of BarkMaxItemSize would leave room for
only two of them on a leaf beside its high key.  Insert may later grow a
built entry up to BarkMaxItemSize.  The meta page's bark_allequalimage
records the rule's result, as nbtree's btm_allequalimage does; only
pageinspect's bark_metap reads it.

A column whose class uses GIN's query interface (bark_index_gin_query) is
exempt from the equalimage half of the rule, as GIN's keys are: GIN finds a
key's entry by its compare procedure and adds the TID to that entry, keeping
the first key image.  bark_index_may_coalesce is the rule insert and build
use: non-unique, no INCLUDE columns, and every key column equalimage except
such a column.  It is sound because nothing returns the stored image as a
row's value (bark_canreturn is false for an extracted column, and a
GIN-interface class has no procedure 8, so no ordering operator reports a
key), and its consistent, comparePartial and triConsistent see the stored
image exactly as GIN's do.  The entry keeps the first row's image:
bark_coalesce_list builds a new LIST or POSTING from the existing entry's
key (bark_single_from_list), not the inserted tuple's, and the build's run
keeps the key of its first, lowest-TID row.  Everything that rewrites an
entry later (the merge pass, VACUUM, bottom-up deletion, ADD_TID and
INSERT_SWAP redo) already takes the key from the stored entry.  The meta
page's bark_allequalimage keeps its meaning (the plain test), so it reads
false for such an index although its keys coalesce.

Version tags
------------

A table AM whose locator is versioned (see "Versioned locators" in
access/amlocator.h) hands each index entry a 4-byte version tag, and an
entry names a row version as (key, TID, tag); 0 means untagged.  BARK
stores, orders and returns tags, and never interprets them.

Which indexes.  The meta page's BARK_META_VERSIONED says the table's
locator was versioned when the index was built; CREATE INDEX and every
rebuild (REINDEX, the rewrite of ALTER TABLE ... SET ACCESS METHOD) set it
from the table's LocatorDesc, and nothing else changes it.  Such an index
stores tags, if it has an extracted column only when its meta page also
has BARK_META_MKTAGS: amindexcanversion (bark_indexcanversion) answers the
executor's index_can_version() from those two bits alone, never from
rd_index, which can change after the build.  A build sets MKTAGS with
VERSIONED for a multikey index that is neither GIN-interface nor, in the
catalog, unique or exclusion (bark_build_mktags); REINDEX recomputes it.
A tagged multikey index's scans settle each entry of a row by the table
AM's verdict ("Versioned multikey scans").  Every other multikey index
returns each row once by TID (the seen set, and a GIN-interface index
scan's TIDBitmap, which carries no tag), which is right only while no row
has two valid entries of different versions, so it stores no tags, and the
executor moves a row whenever an update affects it; its entries stay
untagged, as an index of heap's.  A tagged multikey index's key entries
carry the row's tag, its markers never: a marker names no row, so nothing
could judge its tag (bark_insert_entries forces it to 0).  An index with
BARK_META_VERSIONED
has a pivot suffix reserve (BarkPivotSuffixReserve) of 16 bytes, room for a
pivot's heap TID and tag (6 + 4, MAXALIGNed); every other
index keeps the 8-byte reserve, so an index of a heap table is laid out
byte for byte as before tags existed.  The design text had the reserve grow
to 16 for every index; per index keeps heap's indexes unchanged, which a
page-dump comparison of every block of the R3.1 workload confirms (only the
meta pages differ, in their version word).

Shapes.  t = 0 is never stored: every untagged entry is byte-identical to
the entry of the same shape without tags.  A tagged entry is a SINGLE or an
OVERSIZED entry, never a LIST or POSTING: tagged entries never coalesce.

  tagged SINGLE    the alt-TID bit with BARK_HAS_TAG (0x4000) as the only
                   status bit and zero low offset bits; the t_tid block field
                   holds the trailer offset, the MAXALIGNed end of the key (as
                   a LIST's holds its body offset), and the trailer
                   (BarkTagTrailer) is the heap TID then the tag:
                   MAXALIGN(key) + 16 bytes.  Prefix coding codes its key as
                   a LIST's, with the trailer kept as a body.
  tagged OVERSIZED BARK_HAS_TAG with BARK_IS_OVERFLOW, and the tag after the
                   BarkOverflowRef in a MAXALIGNed 8-byte trailer.  The ref
                   itself has no free bytes (56 bytes with 3 of padding at its
                   end), and a trailer leaves untagged OVERSIZED entries the
                   size they were; the design text had the tag inside the ref.
  tagged pivot     a plain pivot with a heap TID sets BARK_PIVOT_TAG (0x0800,
                   above every attribute count in the low offset bits; the
                   status nibble has no free bit for a pivot) and keeps the
                   tag in the 4 bytes before its heap TID.  An OVERSIZED pivot
                   keeps it as a tagged OVERSIZED leaf entry does.

Every reader of a SINGLE's or OVERSIZED entry's heap TID goes through
BarkEntryGetHeapTid, and of a tag through BarkEntryGetTag, which is 0 for
every untagged shape.

Order.  Entries are ordered by (key, TID, tag), the tag compared as a raw
uint32 (bark_compare_tag): a total order that never changes, untagged
first; only the table AM reads tags modularly, if it likes.  A LIST or
POSTING entry's members are untagged.  bark_compare_itups_tid compares a
(key, TID, tag) with an entry or a pivot.

The order is strict: no two entries share a (key, TID, tag), as no two
share a (key, TID) in an index of heap's.  A table AM may still hand an
insert a (key, TID, tag) the index has: it may give several versions of a
row one tag (heap_versioned tags each with its transaction ID), and an
update that keeps the locator inserts the row's full key set, so A -> B ->
A in one transaction, or a key two updates of a multikey column both keep,
inserts an entry twice.  The second insert finds its copy just before its
insert offset and stores nothing (bark_insert_key); if a scan marked the
copy LP_DEAD, it clears the mark (an OVERWRITE of the same entry), since
the insert says that version is live.  A second copy would break the order
that searches, splits and amcheck rely on, and an index scan would return
the row through both.

Pivots.  Suffix truncation keeps a tag only where it separates: when
lastleft and firstright are equal on every key attribute and on heap TID
(two entries of one row), the high key keeps firstright's TID and tag;
otherwise a pivot has no tag, which is minus infinity on it.  A pivot with
a tag has every key attribute and a heap TID.  A leaf split chooses such a
point (inside a run of one row's entries, equal on key and TID) only when
the page has no other (bark_findsplitloc); the runs are one row's same-key
versions between two VACUUMs, so in practice only a page that is all one
run leaves a tagged pivot.  The right half of a split whose high key has a
tag gets BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED, a "may be tagged" hint on its lower bound,
logged in the split record (XLH_BARK_SPLIT_LLOWTAG/RLOWTAG; the left half
keeps the original page's).  Page deletion moves a page's key space to its
right sibling, which takes the flag with it (XLH_BARK_UNLINK_LOWTAG).  The
flag says only "may", so keeping it is always safe; VACUUM clears it when it
lowers the bound (see "Lowering a lower bound" below), or finds the bound
untagged.

Readers.  Every amgettuple path reports an entry's tag in xs_heaptag: a
page read records each item's tag (BarkScanPosItem.tag, BarkKnnItem.tag),
so forward, backward, SAOP, skip, KNN, mark/restore and parallel scans
return it with the TID.  LIST and POSTING members and every entry of a
multikey index have tag 0.  A bitmap scan drops tags.  The unique check of
an index that stores tags asks table_fetch_tid_version about each equal
entry, with its tag and the index; every other index asks table_fetch_tid.
UNIQUE_CHECK_EXISTING recognizes the row's own entries by TID alone, a stale
one too.  A kill (bark_killitems) names one (TID, tag): an entry is marked
only when its tag is the killed item's.

VACUUM.  With IndexVacuumInfo.version_callback set (a versioned table, an
index that stores tags), VACUUM asks it about every entry, (TID, tag), an
untagged one with tag 0, every LIST or POSTING member too, instead of the
plain callback; without it the plain callback decides by TID, right for a
tagged entry too, since a dead TID means every version at the locator is
dead.  DEAD deletes; LIVE keeps; LIVE_UNTAG lets BARK clear the tag, which
moves the entry from (K, L, t) to (K, L, 0), before every entry of (K, L)
with a tag.  BARK untags an entry only in place (bark_vacuum_untag): moved
to its sorted slot on the same page, in the page's XLOG_BARK_VACUUM record
(nuntagged moves after the deletions; redo makes the same moves).  That is
safe when the slot has an entry before it on the page, or the page is
leftmost, or it lacks BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED: an untagged lower bound <=
(K, L, t) is <= (K, L, 0).  Otherwise (the slot would be the page's first,
under a lower bound that may be (K, L, t0 > 0)) the entry stays tagged and
the verdict is taken as LIVE, counted in VACUUM's DEBUG2 line.  An entry
is never moved to another page: a scan reads one page at a time, and would
miss the row or return it twice.  Instead, after its scan VACUUM lowers the
bound, and untags the entries it kept for it then.

An entry whose (K, L, 0) is already on the page, in the row's own
untagged entry (its twin: a SINGLE, or the last member of the LIST or
POSTING just before it; no entry holds L strictly inside its range, since
a tagged insert divides it at L), stays tagged too, counted as "kept (dup,
AM gave LIVE to an older entry)".  A correct table AM never makes one:
(K, L, t) exists because t's update changed attrs(i), so the twin is
invalid from t on, and with t no newer than the new base it is DEAD in the
same pass.  One verdict is wrong, then.  If it is the twin's LIVE, the
tagged entry is the row's only valid one and deleting it would lose the
row (heapallindexed would not notice: the twin still indexes (K, L)); if
it is the LIVE_UNTAG, keeping costs nothing.  So BARK keeps it, and a count
above 0 means the table AM answered LIVE for an entry the per-index
validity rule makes invalid.  t/004_bark_vacuum.pl builds each twin shape
and shows the row lost under the bark-untag-delete injection point.

Lowering a lower bound.  A leaf P2 whose lower bound is a tagged (K, L, t0),
its left sibling P1's high key and the parent's downlink to P2, can have
an entry (K, L, t) untagged at its start only once the bound is (K, L)
with no tag, minus infinity on the tag.  bark_vacuum_untag records each
entry it kept for the bound, and after the scan and the deletion of empty
leaves, bark_vacuum_lower_bounds calls bark_lower_bound for each such page,
then untags the entries in place under the page's cleanup lock, in an
XLOG_BARK_VACUUM record of their own.  bark_lower_bound:

  * finds the parent's downlink to P2 (bark_find_parent_downlink, which
    returns the parent exclusive-locked) and checks that P1's downlink is
    just before it on the same page, below (K, L), and that P2's is a copy
    of P1's high key;
  * then tries P1's and P2's locks without waiting, as
    bark_delete_empty_leaf does while it holds the parent, since an insert
    that splits a leaf locks the parent after it;
  * under all three locks checks that P1's right link is still P2 and P1
    is not the left half of an incomplete split (whose right half, P2,
    would have no downlink to lower), and that P1 holds no entry at or
    above (K, L), i.e. none of (K, L): the run's entries on P1 must be
    gone.  The rest of what was read is implied, and asserted: only one
    VACUUM runs on an index at a time, and this is it, so no leaf is
    deleted meanwhile; a split of P1 changes P1's right link; nothing else
    rewrites a leaf's high key (a split replaces the page's own, page
    deletion moves only downlinks).  So P1's right link still being P2
    means P1's high key, P2's left link and P2's flag are as read;
  * then rewrites P1's high key and P2's downlink to (K, L) and clears P2's
    BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED, in one record (UNLINK_PAGE's second form, see
    "WAL").

No entry moves, so no reader can miss one or see one twice.  Only the
boundary between P1 and P2 moves, left, from (K, L, t0) to (K, L): the
range [(K, L), (K, L, t0)), entries of row L with key K and a tag below
t0, passes from P1 to P2, and P1 holds none of it.  A reader that read the
old downlink and reaches P1 for a key in that range finds P1's new high
key and moves right to P2 (bark_moveright, Lehman and Yao); one that reads
the new downlink goes to P2.  A scan with no TID in its key (a bound on the
key columns) that lands on P1 reads on to P2, as it would have.  Scans
already on P1 or P2 read P2 or P1 next through the sibling links, which do
not change.  An insert into the range is safe whatever its tag: it holds
its leaf exclusively and checks the leaf's high key under that lock
(bark_moveright), so one that reached P1 before the lowering either
finished there first, and then P1 holds an entry of (K, L) and the lowering
declines, or waits for the lowering's lock and then moves right to P2.  A
correct lowering does not need the table AM to hand out tags in ascending
order; it is when tags do ascend (a counter from the row's high-water mark)
that the run's entries left on P1 are the oldest, the ones VACUUM removes
first, so the lowering succeeds.  Entries of other rows sort below (K, L)
or above (K, L, t0), and other keys likewise, so none is in the range.  The
predicate locks on P1 are copied to P2 (PredicateLockPageSplit), since a
serializable reader that read P1 for the range must conflict with an
insert into it on P2.  The parent's downlinks stay in order: P1's is below
(K, L), checked, and the next one above (K, L, t0).

bark_lower_bound declines, leaving the entry tagged and counted, when a
lock is busy, P1 split since VACUUM's scan (halfway or completely), P1
still holds an entry at or above (K, L) (a live entry of the run, or the
row's own), the bound is an OVERSIZED pivot, P1's downlink is not below
(K, L) (an empty P1 that was not deleted, whose own lower bound is in the
run), or P2 is its parent's first child.  In that last
case the bound is also the high key of the parent's left sibling and a
downlink a level up, and lowering it would have to continue up the levels;
BARK does not.  A tagged bound is left only by a leaf split inside a run
(bark_findsplitloc avoids one whenever the page has another split point),
so such a bound is a parent's first child's about once per fan-out of
tagged bounds.  A later VACUUM tries again, so a busy lock and a live
entry of the run that later dies only delay it.  A page whose flag
outlived its tagged bound (page deletion gave it P1's untagged lower bound,
and kept the flag of the deleted page) has the flag cleared alone, under
P1's and P2's locks, left to right, as a split takes them, without the
parent.  VACUUM's DEBUG2 line counts the bounds lowered, and a DEBUG2 line
for each one kept gives the reason.

Deletion.  Simple and bottom-up deletion put each entry's tag in its
TM_IndexStatus, and offer a TID once per entry: the table AM's verdict is
about the entry.  Before a leaf of an index that stores tags splits, and
after simple and bottom-up deletion, bark_tagged_delete offers the page's
tagged SINGLEs to table_index_delete_tuples, as a bottom-up pass in which
all are promising, so stale entries of live rows go (R3.3 of the RethinkDB
review).

Version 2.  An index without BARK_META_VERSIONED, every version 2 index
among them, answers false to amindexcanversion, so the executor moves the
row of a versioned table whenever an update affects the index, and never
hands it a tag (bark_check_can_tag reports one as an internal error).  A
version 2 index on a versioned table, which only pg_upgrade of a
pre-version-3 BARK index could produce, then works as an index of heap's
does; REINDEX makes it store tags.

Conversion.  A multikey index with BARK_META_MKTAGS that the catalog
later calls unique or exclusion (a constraint added after the build, as
DocumentDB's collMod prepareUnique adds one) keeps the bit and its tagged
entries, which the table AM still judges and rule D still reads.  But it
answers false to amindexmaykeeplocator (bark_indexmaykeeplocator, read
from rd_index at run time), so an update that needs new entries in it
moves the row, and a constraint check never meets two versions at one
locator through an update that began after the change.  An update whose
statement began before it can still keep the locator: DocumentDB's
prepareUnique holds only AccessShareLock on the table, so nothing waits
for it.  While that update is in progress, a dirty snapshot sees two
versions at its locator, v0 (being replaced) and v1, each reached only
through its own entries.  Rule D's R, keyed by the locator, would return
the first the scan reaches and skip the rest: with v0 = {1,2}, v1 =
{2,3} and a check for {2,3} under EXCLUDE (doc WITH =), the stale
(2,L,0) returns v0, its recheck fails, and v1 is never compared, so a
duplicate commits.  So a scan under a non-MVCC snapshot of a multikey
index with MKTAGS whose rd_index says unique or exclusion
(BarkMkScanState.unsettled, set in bark_mk_rescan) puts no row in R but
one: the row this backend's transaction inserted last into the index
(bark_is_last_insert), which is the row a check right after its insert is
about, and which core must not meet twice ("found self tuple multiple
times").  That row has one version a dirty snapshot sees, since nobody
else can update a row whose insert is in progress.  Every other entry is
judged by itself, the table AM returns the version that entry is valid
for, and every row comes back with xs_recheck, since a version need not
hold the key that reached it.  A check of a row other than the last
inserted (a deferred exclusion recheck, or the check after a HOT or
kept-locator update, which inserted nothing) can then meet its own row
twice and raise that error: a refused statement, not a wrong answer.  The
rule holds until VACUUM's barrier has passed (BARK_META_SETTLED, below),
read from the meta page at each rescan.

The meta page records the conversion in two bits beside MKTAGS.
BARK_META_CONVERTING says the conversion has begun: the first BARK call
that sees MKTAGS and an rd_index unique or exclusion sets it
(bark_note_conversion: the first insert of a statement, through
bark_insert_state; the non-MVCC rescan of a constraint check; VACUUM's
bulkdelete, or its cleanup when bulkdelete did not run), under the meta
page's exclusive lock, logged whole, as BARK_META_MULTIKEY is.  Nothing
in recovery sets it.  From then on bark_indexmaykeeplocator answers false
whatever rd_index says, so a constraint dropped mid-conversion does not
let updates keep locators again: the barrier must see every writer that
could still add a tagged entry.  amindexcanversion stays MKTAGS: an update
that began before the conversion and keeps its locator still needs its
entries tagged.  BARK_META_SETTLED says VACUUM's barrier has passed, so
no update that began under the old rd_index is still in progress.
CONVERTING without MKTAGS, and SETTLED without CONVERTING, are corruption,
which amcheck and the page validator report (bark_verify_meta; the
injection points bark-verify-fault-converting and -settled plant them, on
the primary and in redo).

bark_index_converting's answer is read again in each transaction
(BarkAmCache.convlxid), never kept longer: the bit is set outside any
catalog change, so no invalidation tells other backends.  That is enough
for the barrier.  A transaction that read the bit clear may keep locators
until it ends, but it took its lock on the table first, and the bit was
set after; a later transaction reads it set.  A backend that set the bit
for a catalog change that then aborted leaves it set: the conversion runs
to its end anyway, as after a constraint dropped mid-way.

The barrier (bark_convert_barrier) runs in VACUUM: in bulkdelete, and in
cleanup when this transaction has not tried it (no bulkdelete ran, or a
parallel worker ran it; workers do not run it, since they would wait for
the leader's own lock).  It waits out every transaction holding a lock on
the table that conflicts with ShareLock, WaitForLockers as CREATE INDEX
CONCURRENTLY waits out writers (bark_convert_wait; the injection point
bark-convert-barrier-wait is before the wait).  Every update that may keep
a locator under the old rd_index holds RowExclusiveLock until its
transaction ends; VACUUM's own ShareUpdateExclusiveLock is not counted
(GetLockConflicts skips the caller).  An autovacuum worker does not wait,
since an idle-in-transaction writer could hold it indefinitely: it asks
GetLockConflicts and passes only when the answer is empty
(bark-convert-autovac-busy marks the other case); the next VACUUM tries
again.  A barrier that passes for the first time sets SETTLED, then waits
once more: an insert stores entries untagged only once it has read
SETTLED (BarkInsertState.untag, read once per statement), so a statement
that read the meta page just before may still add a tagged one, and the
second wait outlasts it.  Every later VACUUM waits again before it counts.
The barrier runs once per index and transaction, which a VACUUM of one
table is.

From SETTLED on, inserts store their entries untagged, even when the table
AM hands a tag: every update moves the rows it changes (CONVERTING), and a
new locator has no history, so an untagged entry is valid for it.  This
replaces the design's "no tagged entry can be added after SETTLED", which
did not hold: canversion stays MKTAGS, and the executor tags every entry
of an index that can store tags, moved row or not.

Completion (bark_convert_complete).  A VACUUM pass that passed the
barrier counts the tagged leaf entries it leaves (BarkVacState.ntagged:
those it neither deleted nor untagged, and minus those bark_lower_bound
then untagged).  The pass deletes (cleanup locks, splits followed by cycle
ID), so the count is exact; a cleanup with nothing to delete uses a
callback that keeps every entry, with the table AM's version callback if
it gave one.  With none left it clears MKTAGS, CONVERTING and SETTLED in
one meta page write, and invalidates the index's relcache entry, since
every backend caches MKTAGS.  The index is then one that stores no tags:
amindexcanversion answers false, the executor moves rows, and amcheck
expects no tagged leaf entry.  Tagged pivots may remain, where
bark_lower_bound could not lower a parent's first child's bound; they
still separate the entries below them correctly, so amcheck and the
validator accept a pivot tag in any index with BARK_META_VERSIONED.  An
entry kept tagged because its page's lower bound has a tag, or because the
table AM keeps answering LIVE for it (a row with history the table AM
cannot drop), keeps the conversion open; until it ends the index works
as a converting one, item 5's rule included until SETTLED, and rule D
after.

Tests: bark_mk_versioned (T12a: CONVERTING, SETTLED, untagged inserts,
completion, the constraint after it; the bits through each caller of
bark_note_conversion); isolation bark_mk_convert (T12b: VACUUM waits for
the in-place update, then SETTLED); t/009_bark_convert.pl (autovacuum
does not wait, a manual VACUUM waits twice, a row the table AM will not
untag holds the conversion open, completion, and a standby that sees each
bit and returns the same rows, under wal_consistency_checking);
t/008_bark_mktags.pl (T12f).

Updates.  An update that keeps the locator inserts, into each index it
affects, the new version's entries, tagged.  For a multikey index that is
the new value's full key set, unchanged keys included: the old entries are
invalid for the new version once the column changed (the table AM's rule
is per index, not per key), so `doc && '{b}'` after {a,b} became {b,c}
finds the row through (b,L,new).  The old entries stay until VACUUM
removes them.  barkcomparedatums still tells the executor when a value
gives the same entries ({a,b} to {b,a} on a class that sorts keys), and
then nothing is inserted.

Writers.  barkhandler sets amcanversion, amindexcanversion and
amindexmaykeeplocator.  aminsert
and the build callback read IndexInfo.ii_VersionTag (the executor, or the
table AM's build and validation scans, set it).  A tagged row's entry is
placed by (key, TID, tag), as a tagged SINGLE or a tagged OVERSIZED entry,
and never coalesces: bark_coalesce_list skips a tagged
neighbour and a tagged insert, the merge pass (bark_merge_page) ends a group
at a tagged entry, bark_load writes one as it is and ends the run of its key
there, and VACUUM's and bottom-up deletion's re-forms touch only LIST and
POSTING entries.  A tagged row's
(key, TID) can fall inside the TID range of an untagged LIST or POSTING of
its key; the entry sorts after the members up to its TID and before the
rest, so that entry is divided there first (bark_entry_divide, logged as
INSERT_SWAP with the tag after the stride, which redo reads as "divide, add
nothing"), then the tagged entry goes in between by INSERT_LEAF, or, when
the parts do not fit, the page splits with the parts in place of the entry
and the insert descends again.  CREATE INDEX sorts a tagged row's entry
with the rest: the build's sort (bark_sort_begin, a tuplesort variant of
BARK's own) holds tagged SINGLEs and orders by (key, heap TID, tag),
reading the heap TID and tag from the trailer, where nbtree's sort would
read t_tid.  bark_load packs them to the fill factor as it does untagged
entries.  A leaf the load ends inside a run of entries equal on key and
heap TID (one row's entries of one key and different tags, which a build
sees only from a table AM that hands it a row's older versions) moves the
run's part on that leaf to the next one, unless the part fills the page or
takes more than a quarter of it (bark_build_run_tail), as a split avoids
such a cut; a leaf whose lower bound keeps a tag gets
BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED.  The quarter bounds what a leaf gives up, so that
the next leaf has room for the part, the entry that did not fit and one
more (each at most BarkMaxItemSize).  A split chooses among all of a
page's split points and avoids a run whenever the page has another; the
load fills leaves in order, so it cuts a run longer than a quarter of a
page where the leaf reaches its fill target, which costs only what the
untag rule (above) keeps tagged.  t/002_bark_tags.pl in test_versioned_tableam builds both
cases (its build_history setting hands each tagged row's older versions),
and an injection point, bark-build-ignore-runs, turns the move off.  A
tagged row whose key is too large to sort goes
in by the second pass (bark_build_oversized_pass), as a tagged OVERSIZED
entry; serial and parallel builds alike, since the leader's second scan
sees the table AM's tags.  Every WAL record carries
entry images or re-forms only LIST and POSTING entries, so tagged entries
need no other record change.

The page validator and amcheck check the shapes (BARK_HAS_TAG only on a
SINGLE or OVERSIZED entry, the trailer at the MAXALIGNed end of the key and
ending the entry, a tag never 0, no tag in an index not built for tags), the
pivot rule (a tagged pivot has every key attribute and a heap TID), the
(key, TID, tag) order on a page and against the high key and across
downlinks, that a version 2 meta page does not claim
BARK_META_VERSIONED, and that BARK_META_MKTAGS comes with
BARK_META_VERSIONED (redo too, from the page alone) on an index with an
extracted column that is not GIN-interface (with the index at hand), and
that no marker is tagged.  An index that is unique or exclusion now but has
MKTAGS is not corrupt: rd_index can change after the build.  amcheck also checks that a leaf whose lower bound has a
tag has BARK_LOWBOUND_TAGGED, and the validator that only a leaf with a left
sibling has it.  pageinspect's bark_page_items shows a tag column, and
bark_page_stats the flag as lowbound_tagged.

Invariants
----------

  * Block 0 is the meta page; its bark_magic is BARK_MAGIC and its
    bark_version is between BARK_MIN_VERSION and BARK_VERSION.
  * In an index with an extracted column, each (key, TID) is one entry
    member.  The heap TID (InvalidBlockNumber, 1) is a marker's, only on a
    SINGLE entry of an index whose procedure 7 can return markers, with
    every key column but the extracted one NULL.
  * Every key column but an extracted one is ordered by an operator class
    in a btree operator family (BARK declares ambtreeopfamilies); an
    extracted column's keys are ordered by its class's procedure 1, in an
    operator family of BARK's own.
  * On a leaf page, entries are in key order; within one key, LIST locators
    are ascending and a POSTING set is in sbm's canonical encoding.  Entries of
    one key have disjoint TID ranges in ascending order, across pages too.
  * The alt-TID status bits are mutually exclusive in what they encode: an
    entry is exactly one of SINGLE, LIST, POSTING, OVERSIZED, or PIVOT;
    BARK_HAS_TAG alone is a tagged SINGLE, with BARK_IS_OVERFLOW a tagged
    OVERSIZED entry, and on a PIVOT the same bit is BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID
    (see "Version tags").
  * Leaf entries are in (key, TID, tag) order; only a SINGLE or OVERSIZED
    entry of an index with BARK_META_VERSIONED, and with BARK_META_MKTAGS
    if it has an extracted column, has a tag, never 0; a marker never has
    one.  BARK_META_MKTAGS implies BARK_META_VERSIONED.
  * An OVERSIZED entry's full tuple lives on a chain of BARK_OVERFLOW pages
    (bark_flags has BARK_OVERFLOW, not BARK_LEAF): each page holds up to
    BarkOverflowChunkSize raw bytes of the tuple and links to the next chunk
    via bark_next (BARK_P_NONE at the tail); the chunks concatenate to the full
    tuple.  Overflow pages carry no items and are skipped by the leaf scans
    (vacuum, cleanup) and by amcheck's per-page checks.
  * bark_page_id is BARK_PAGE_ID on every BARK page.
  * No leaf entry is larger than BarkMaxItemSize: a third of the page, but no
    more than an index tuple's size field holds (INDEX_SIZE_MASK), which
    bounds it with 32 kB blocks.
  * A high key is a pivot with between one and all of the index's key
    attributes, and no INCLUDE attributes, and a heap TID only with all of
    them.  Every key on its page is less than or equal to it, and strictly
    less when attributes were removed; a leaf entry equal to it is below its
    heap TID.  Every key on the right sibling is greater than or equal to it,
    by (key, lowest TID) on a leaf.
  * A downlink is a pivot with at most the index's key attributes, the first
    downlink of a level has none, and the first key on the child it points
    at is greater than or equal to it, by (key, lowest TID) on a leaf.
  * A POSTING entry is at least as large as BarkPostingEntrySize of its key
    and of sbm_removal_bound of its set.  An sbm is not size-monotone:
    removing members from a dense run turns all-ones vectors (a 2-bit flag)
    into stored mixed vectors and splits RLE runs into sparse chunks, so the
    survivors of a VACUUM can need more bytes than the whole set did.  The
    removal bound of a subset never exceeds that of the set, so when VACUUM
    re-encodes an entry's survivors (as a POSTING sized for their own bound, a
    LIST if that is smaller, or a SINGLE) the result fits the entry's slot,
    and VACUUM rewrites it in place in the page's one WAL record, never
    splitting a page or moving locators to another entry.  POSTING entries
    written before this reserve existed (with no length word) are not
    readable; BARK has not been released, so there is no version bump.
